  var DATA = {"levels":[{"ru":"Легко","en":"Easy","cefr":"A1–A2"},{"ru":"Средне","en":"Medium","cefr":"A2–B1"},{"ru":"Сложно","en":"Hard","cefr":"B1"},{"ru":"Очень сложно","en":"Very hard","cefr":"B1–B2"}],"listen":[[{"t":"Tom's Sunday","a":"l1-01.mp3","d":35,"w":"tomqjpj2lonmpd28zjou9ivtpstl22psofyp2trnqg2uqaqsqm422lsfaqrsq9msupvp823rpop22wsrmmclpqjxh2nupps4","p":["On Sunday, Tom gets up at 10 o'clock. Then he reads his newspaper in the kitchen. He has breakfast at 11.30 and then he telephones his mother in Scotland.","In the afternoon, at 1.00, Tom plays tennis with his sister and after that, they eat dinner in a restaurant. At 6.00, Tom swims for one hour and then he goes by bike to his brother's house. They talk and listen to music.","Tom watches television in the evening and drinks a glass of warm milk. He goes to bed at 11.30."],"q":[["Tom gets up at … on Sunday.",["9 o'clock","10 o'clock","11 o'clock","11.30"],1],["Tom reads his newspaper in …",["bed","the living room","the kitchen","the garden"],2],["Tom telephones his … in Scotland.",["father","sister","brother","mother"],3],["What time does Tom play tennis?",["At 11.30","At 12.00","At 1.00","At 6.00"],2],["Who does Tom play tennis with?",["His mother","His sister","His brother","His friend"],1],["After tennis, Tom and his sister ….",["go shopping","eat dinner in a restaurant","visit their mother","listen to music"],1],["How does Tom go to his brother's house?",["By car","By bus","By bike","By train"],2],["What does Tom drink before going to bed?",["A cup of tea","A glass of water","A glass of warm milk","A cup of coffee"],2]]},{"t":"Train Announcement","a":"l1-02.mp3","d":44,"w":"wvihuzobjtoplbsuupfepmnqtknksgmnpvnm9nuxqpgln82msskoqgiacrkunspknivio6qqlmkbtomnlnq2poroomm2jsm8","p":["Good afternoon, passengers. We have some important information.","The 2:30 train to London is cancelled. We are very sorry for the inconvenience.","The train is cancelled because one of the train drivers is sick today and can't come to work.","The next train to London will arrive at 3:15. It will now depart from Platform 5, not Platform 4. Please go to Platform 5 if you want the train to London, thank you.","The train to Manchester will arrive at 3:00 on Platform 2.","Tomorrow is Easter Sunday. There are no trains before 10:00 AM. The first train leaves at 10:15 from Platform 1. Please check the schedule for more information about our Easter service. Thank you for your understanding."],"q":[["The 2:30 train to ... is cancelled.",["Manchester","London","Liverpool","Edinburgh"],1],["The train is cancelled because the driver is ...",["late","on holiday","sick","busy"],2],["The next train to London will arrive at ...",["2:30","3:00","3:15","4:00"],2],["The London train will depart from Platform ...",["1","2","4","5"],3],["The train to ... will arrive at 3:00.",["London","Manchester","Liverpool","Edinburgh"],1],["Tomorrow is ... Sunday.",["Easter","Christmas","Palm","Holiday"],0],["On Easter Sunday, there are no trains before ...",["9:00 AM","10:00 AM","10:15 AM","11:00 AM"],1],["The first train on Easter Sunday leaves from Platform ...",["1","2","4","5"],0]]},{"t":"Thomas and Cynthia","a":"l1-03.mp3","d":50,"w":"vqvo9hvvgoqrsk92rvtwagtypemxqmo2rs2snyj2vswkpsp2rqlxnyv2mpyrnqcjpskesqon2pusnqz9nhtfkttitvj6luul","p":["Thomas likes his job at the post office. He works Monday to Friday. He likes helping people with letters and packages. He comes to work at 8:00 every morning.","Thomas has a problem with Cynthia. Cynthia works at the post office too. She is always late. She comes at 8:30 or 9:00. Cynthia doesn't do her work. She drinks tea and talks a lot. She talks about TV, her garden, and her dog Carlos.","When Cynthia doesn't work, Thomas has to do extra work. He stays late to finish everything.","One day, Thomas says to Cynthia, \"Please help more!\" Cynthia says, \"Sorry,\" but the next day, she is late again.","Thomas likes his job, but he doesn't like working with Cynthia. He thinks about getting a new job."],"q":[["Where does Thomas work?",["At a school","At a post office","At a hospital","At a restaurant"],1],["Thomas works from Monday to ...",["Thursday","Friday","Saturday","Sunday"],1],["What time does Thomas come to work?",["7:00","7:30","8:00","8:30"],2],["Cynthia is always ...",["happy","sad","angry","late"],3],["What is the name of Cynthia's dog?",["Tommy","Carlos","Rex","Max"],1],["Cynthia likes to talk about ...",["books","movies","TV","music"],2],["Thomas sometimes has to ... because of Cynthia.",["come early","stay late","work at home","miss work"],1],["Thomas is thinking about ...",["getting a new job","talking to the manager","working with Cynthia more","coming to work later"],0]]},{"t":"Corper Castle","a":"l1-04.mp3","d":54,"w":"zolsmqkvrilrujlmnqhrpmlwqs2rsmo2nvrqqksopfotqor9spqnrpqrxgssrsnlkumnq8nsurqoosnprmr6prnhgvnpmsqo","p":["Hello! Welcome to Corper Castle! My name is Sarah. I am your guide today.","Corper Castle is a big, pretty house. It is not a real castle. It looks like a castle because it has tall towers and big windows. But it is a home, not a fortress. The castle is over 200 years old.","You can visit many rooms inside. You can see the great hall, dining room, and library. The library has many old books. The furniture is very old too, but still good.","There is a big garden around Corper Castle. It has many flowers, trees, and a small pond. You can walk in the garden after our tour.","Many people come here to learn about history and see the beautiful gardens. People also have weddings here.","Now, let's start our tour. Please follow me to the first room."],"q":[["The name of the guide at Corper Castle is ...",["Mary","Sarah","Jane","Anna"],1],["Corper Castle is actually a ...",["real castle","fortress","big house","small cottage"],2],["The age of Corper Castle is ...",["100 years","150 years","200 years","over 200 years"],3],["Inside the castle, visitors can see the ...",["bedroom","kitchen","library","bathroom"],2],["The library contains many ...",["old books","paintings","weapons","statues"],0],["Around Corper Castle there is a ...",["parking lot","big garden","playground","market"],1],["In the garden visitors can find a ...",["swimming pool","small pond","fountain","waterfall"],1],["People come to Corper Castle for ...",["shopping","weddings","concerts","sports"],1]]},{"t":"My School Years","a":"l1-05.mp3","d":58,"w":"yttttsuuqqnjtupqnmyrvwpoluuqnuovqto2owltrpuqtqvr9toywqpvrscvvkprrqothsuuwqszusqhvsotrqrqvurxspzc","p":["In January 1990, I was at primary school and in January 2000, I was at university! I left primary school in September 1990 and went to a secondary school five kilometres from my house. I got the bus every day at 7.30am and didn't get home until 5 o'clock in the evening. What a difficult life!","My family bought a satellite TV in 1991 and a new video the next year. In 1994, my brother bought a computer and I got a bicycle the next year.","I left my school in 1997 but decided not to go to university like most people: instead, I went walking in France alone for four months and then drove around America for three months with my brother. For the first six months of 1998, I got a job in a health food shop and then went to university in October 1998 in Wales."],"q":[["In January 1990, the person was at ...",["university","primary school","secondary school","work"],1],["The distance from home to secondary school was ...",["two kilometres","three kilometres","five kilometres","seven kilometres"],2],["The time when the person arrived home from school was ...",["3 o'clock","4 o'clock","5 o'clock","6 o'clock"],2],["The person's family bought a satellite TV in ...",["1990","1991","1992","1994"],1],["The person got a ... in 1995.",["computer","bicycle","new video","car"],1],["After leaving school in 1997, the person went to ...",["university","France","America","Wales"],1],["The person traveled around America with ...",["friends","family","brother","alone"],2],["The person started university in ...",["1997","1998","1999","2000"],1]]},{"t":"A Phone Message","a":"l1-06.mp3","d":37,"w":"vwrpuuqqprfvunsuhnpi3rssutj3qoqqnkiuzjvmtjiqkqjugsvootqtxmkor5rvputkpvwnrnygtprszqomqrrkqnxqqsta","p":["Hi, John, this is Marina Silva calling from Old Time Toys. Your colleague Alex gave me your phone number. She said you can help me.","I need some information on your new products. Could you please call me when you are back in the office? My phone number is 0-2-0-8, 6-5-5-7-6-2-1.","Also, can you please email me your new brochure and information about your prices? My email address is Marina, that's M-A-R-I-N-A, dot Silva, S-I-L-V-A, at O-L-D-T-I-M-E hyphen toys dot com.","Thanks a lot. I look forward to hearing from you."],"q":[["The caller's name is ...",["John","Marina","Alex","Silva"],1],["Marina works at ...",["New Time Toys","Old Time Toys","Good Time Toys","All Time Toys"],1],["Marina got John's phone number from ...",["the internet","his website","his colleague","a newspaper"],2],["Marina wants information about ...",["old products","new products","delivery times","job opportunities"],1],["Marina's phone number is ...",["0208 6557261","0208 6557621","0208 6557612","0208 6557162"],1],["Marina would like John to send her a ...",["letter","catalog","brochure","sample"],2],["Marina's email address contains a ...",["dot","hyphen","underscore","slash"],1],["The domain of Marina's email address is ...",["oldtime-toys.com","oldtimetoys.com","old-timetoys.com","old-time-toys.com"],0]]},{"t":"Summer Plans","a":"l1-07.mp3","d":39,"w":"tlqrrqms2xqsspmrcptrnubmtqosvdunpsqqt2vlmkpqsu7burmzfntrf2sodolliuwoulrsornmneixpmujoijqrmmmllq2","p":["I usually teach English at a language school in the summer. I often can't enjoy the summer because I am so busy. So this summer, I'm not planning to work. Instead, I'm going to have a proper summer holiday. I'm going to buy a campervan and drive around Ireland. I'm going to visit lots of beautiful beaches and learn to surf! I'm mostly going to travel alone, but I will visit friends. One friend, Cathy, is a school teacher, so she has a long summer holiday, so hopefully, we will spend a week or two together. Another Irish friend, Joe, has a new house there, so I'll stay with him for a few days and help him paint the rooms."],"q":[["The person usually teaches ... in the summer.",["Math","English","History","Science"],1],["This summer, the person is planning to ...",["work at a language school","have a proper holiday","study at university","stay at home"],1],["The person is going to buy a ... for the trip.",["car","tent","campervan","bicycle"],2],["The person is planning to travel around ...",["Scotland","England","Wales","Ireland"],3],["During the trip, the person wants to learn to ...",["swim","surf","fish","sail"],1],["The person will travel ... for most of the trip.",["with family","with friends","alone","with Cathy"],2],["The person's friend Cathy is a ...",["doctor","school teacher","language teacher","police officer"],1],["At Joe's house, the person will help him ...",["build furniture","clean the garden","paint the rooms","fix the roof"],2]]},{"t":"My Small House","a":"l1-08.mp3","d":47,"w":"worj2posjrhlfqe2rotdqq6trinmxaxlqp8zprk5quoueq8tvvqowlcotwbnuvdzbtl2nrmaymqgrqoobprrqpoajuqryoq4","p":["I live in a small house in the city. My house has two bedrooms, a living room, a kitchen, and a bathroom.","My bedroom is not very big, but it's comfortable. I have a bed, a desk, and a wardrobe. There are some pictures on the walls.","The living room is my favorite room. It has a sofa, two armchairs, and a TV. There is also a big window with a nice view of the garden.","The kitchen is small but modern. I have a refrigerator, a stove, and a microwave. I like cooking in my kitchen.","The garden is behind the house. It's small but beautiful. I have some flowers and a small table with chairs. In summer, I like to sit in the garden and read books."],"q":[["The speaker lives in a ... house.",["big","small","new","old"],1],["The house is in ...",["the city","the countryside","a village","a suburb"],0],["The house has ... bedrooms.",["one","two","three","four"],1],["In the speaker's bedroom, there is a ...",["sofa","TV","desk","bookshelf"],2],["The speaker's favorite room is the ...",["bedroom","living room","kitchen","bathroom"],1],["In the living room, there are ...",["two sofas","three armchairs","two armchairs","four chairs"],2],["The kitchen is ...",["big and old","big and modern","small and old","small and modern"],3],["In the garden, the speaker likes to ...",["grow vegetables","play games","have parties","read books"],3]]},{"t":"My Favourite Season","a":"l1-09.mp3","d":35,"w":"mmmjnhgooknnrfl2njvklm7dnmglkkjsnb2kngziggpb2okkem2nsnmka2pqhpkn2ipqog2dmkjmmhknqd2sknldlp2mon32","p":["My favorite season is summer. I like summer because the weather is warm and sunny. The days are long, and the nights are short.","In summer, I can go swimming in the sea or in a pool. I can also go to the park and have picnics with my friends. I like to eat ice cream in the summer.","I often go on vacation in the summer. Last year, I went to Spain with my family. We stayed in a hotel near the beach. We swam in the sea every day and ate delicious food.","Summer is a time for relaxing and having fun. That's why I like it so much."],"q":[["The speaker's favorite season is ...",["spring","summer","autumn","winter"],1],["The speaker likes this season because ...",["it's cold","it's warm","it rains a lot","it snows"],1],["In summer, the speaker can go ...",["skiing","ice skating","swimming","sledding"],2],["The speaker likes to eat ... in the summer.",["hot soup","hot chocolate","ice cream","cake"],2],["Last year, the speaker went to ... on vacation.",["France","Italy","Spain","Portugal"],2],["The speaker stayed in a hotel near the ...",["mountains","city center","forest","beach"],3],["During the vacation, the speaker ... every day.",["went hiking","visited museums","went swimming","went shopping"],2],["The speaker thinks summer is a time for ...",["working hard","studying","staying indoors","relaxing and having fun"],3]]},{"t":"My Hobby Is Cooking","a":"l1-10.mp3","d":36,"w":"qtll2lpopmor2qvuko2oto3mppr8nmwomol82rrmnm2sznlmf2rqlkrrqlp6gmqpudgspnsnj2qrlqhss2jpttxwmj2oqonb","p":["My hobby is cooking. I started cooking when I was 15 years old. My grandmother taught me how to cook. She was a very good cook.","I cook dinner every day. I like to try new recipes from different countries. Italian food is my favorite. I love making pasta and pizza.","On weekends, I sometimes invite my friends to dinner. They say my food is very good. I'm happy when people enjoy my cooking.","I want to take cooking classes to learn more. Maybe one day I will open my own restaurant. That would be my dream job."],"q":[["The speaker's hobby is ...",["reading","cooking","painting","photography"],1],["The speaker started this hobby at the age of ...",["10","12","15","18"],2],["Who taught the speaker how to cook?",["Mother","Father","Grandmother","Friend"],2],["The speaker cooks dinner ...",["sometimes","on weekends","once a week","every day"],3],["The speaker's favorite type of food is ...",["French","Italian","Chinese","Mexican"],1],["The speaker loves making ...",["cakes and cookies","soups and salads","bread and pastries","pasta and pizza"],3],["On weekends, the speaker sometimes ...",["goes to restaurants","takes cooking classes","invites friends to dinner","watches cooking shows"],2],["In the future, the speaker would like to ...",["become a chef","open a restaurant","write a cookbook","travel and learn about food"],1]]}],[{"t":"Surfing Instructor","a":"l2-01.mp3","d":43,"w":"mmrrnj5pmomju2mr6qsmoqw5oohqtzpe2tplnpjinim22pt2vnht9kulwoin2mjmrqrsms22ysmifsm2numpg2qzl2tytona","p":["Last year, I did a surfing instructor course, and I spent the summer teaching people to surf. It was great! I was in the sea and sun all day, and every evening, my friends and I had a barbecue on the beach. The surf school asked me to return this summer, but I'm not going to do that. Last autumn, I learned to scuba dive, and I'm going to do a diving instructor course soon. Then, I plan to teach diving all summer to earn some money. I don't want to stay in the UK. It's too cold here! I want to fly to Greece or Egypt. I'll go there alone. I'm sure I will meet lots of cool people there!"],"q":[["The speaker worked as a ... instructor last year.",["diving","surfing","swimming","sailing"],1],["Last summer, the speaker had ... on the beach every evening.",["a party","a barbecue","a picnic","a concert"],1],["The surf school wants the speaker to ... this summer.",["come back","pay more","study more","bring friends"],0],["In autumn, the speaker learned to ...",["windsurf","scuba dive","sail boats","ski"],1],["The speaker's next course will be for ...",["surfing teacher","diving instructor","beach lifeguard","boat captain"],1],["This summer, the speaker wants to earn money by teaching ...",["surfing","diving","swimming","sailing"],1],["The speaker thinks the UK is too ...",["expensive","busy","cold","far"],2],["The speaker plans to go to ... or ...",["Spain / Portugal","France / Italy","Greece / Egypt","USA / Canada"],2]]},{"t":"Cycling with Alfie","a":"l2-02.mp3","d":43,"w":"wouoppni2tqsrsvqn5zrut2pruvunhipxo2xsmhvttvpcupvsuegzouppmq2usrnduslnpr3pth8uqskm5stli2qytjsrrvh","p":["Last month, my friend Alfie and I had a crazy idea. We decided to cycle from the south of England to the north of Scotland, from Land's End to John o'Groats. It's an 874-mile trip! It takes ten to fourteen days. But I'm not a fast cyclist, and I want to enjoy visiting different places, so we plan to ride for four weeks. We will visit historic cities like Bath and Edinburgh, cycle through the pretty countryside and visit museums and castles on the way. Alfie wanted to camp, but I'd like a comfortable bed at the end of each day, so we are going to stay in guest houses. I hope it doesn't rain a lot, but I hope it's not too hot either!"],"q":[["The speaker and Alfie decided to cycle across ... last month.",["Wales","England","Scotland","England and Scotland"],3],["The total distance of their planned trip is ... miles.",["478","784","847","874"],3],["The cycling trip normally takes between ... days.",["5 to 7","7 to 10","10 to 14","14 to 20"],2],["The speaker plans to complete the trip in ... weeks.",["two","three","four","five"],2],["During the trip, they will visit cities like Bath and ...",["London","Glasgow","Edinburgh","Manchester"],2],["Along the way, they plan to visit ...",["friends and family","museums and castles","parks and gardens","shops and restaurants"],1],["For accommodation, they decided to stay in ...",["tents","hotels","guest houses","with friends"],2],["The speaker hopes the weather will not be too ...",["rainy or hot","windy or cold","foggy or wet","sunny or warm"],0]]},{"t":"Weather Forecast","a":"l2-03.mp3","d":61,"w":"slnrjkrorjqpodwpkopwnplotsloksrns2wtndtnsupn2onlupnol8ozkmps8rqqtdnpranqiisionmm9ooqtr9vpscqqrpc","p":["Good morning, listeners. Here's today's weather update. We're expecting a mostly cloudy day with temperatures around 20 degrees. There's a moderate chance of rain in the evening, so you might want to bring an umbrella if you're planning to be out late.","Tomorrow will be slightly cooler, with temperatures dropping to about 15 degrees. The wind will be coming from the east, and humidity levels will remain moderate throughout the day.","Looking ahead to the weekend, we're forecasting much better conditions. It will be sunny and warm, with temperatures reaching 25 degrees. It's a perfect time to plan outdoor activities, but don't forget your sunglasses!","For those of you headed to the beach, the water temperature is quite pleasant at 22 degrees. The wind will be light, and there's no sign of any storms.","That's all for today's weather forecast. Stay tuned for traffic updates coming up next."],"q":[["Today's temperature will be around ... degrees.",["15","20","25","30"],1],["The weather will be ...",["sunny","cloudy","rainy","windy"],1],["Tomorrow will be ... than today.",["warmer","cooler","the same temperature","much colder"],1],["There is a chance of rain in the ...",["morning","afternoon","evening","night"],2],["The weekend will be ...",["sunny and hot","cloudy and cool","rainy and windy","foggy and cold"],0],["People should bring ... for the afternoon.",["an umbrella","sunglasses","a warm jacket","gloves"],0],["The wind will be coming from the ...",["north","south","east","west"],2],["The humidity level will be ...",["low","moderate","high","extremely high"],1]]},{"t":"My Daily Routine","a":"l2-04.mp3","d":71,"w":"pqrtoduoxwkrvlurwhtmnfoomrlmoxtoiswlufuqsjrntmntjuuptrnopzxsljpqrqpnnogmltowfpspsilnqvgmsvspkzqm","p":["Hi there! I thought I'd share my typical daily routine with you. I usually wake up at 6:30 in the morning when my alarm goes off. I'm not really a morning person, but I've learned to get up right away.","After a quick shower, I have breakfast. I typically have toast with jam and a cup of coffee. Then I get dressed and leave for work at around 7:30. I usually take the bus to work, which takes about 20 minutes.","I work in an office as an administrative assistant. My workday starts at 8:30 and ends at 5:00. For lunch, I usually bring food from home and eat in the office cafeteria with my colleagues.","After work, I like to go for a walk in the park near my house to get some fresh air. When I get home, I make dinner, which is usually something simple like pasta or a salad. After dinner, I might watch TV or read a book.","I try to get to bed by 10:30 PM so I can get enough sleep. On weekends, my routine is more relaxed. I sleep in until about 8:00 and enjoy spending time with my family or going to the movies with friends."],"q":[["The speaker wakes up at ...",["5:30 AM","6:00 AM","6:30 AM","7:00 AM"],2],["For breakfast, the speaker usually has ...",["cereal","eggs","toast","fruit"],2],["The speaker goes to work by ...",["car","bus","train","bicycle"],1],["The speaker works as a ...",["teacher","doctor","office worker","shop assistant"],2],["For lunch, the speaker usually ...",["eats at home","brings food from home","eats in a cafeteria","skips lunch"],1],["After work, the speaker likes to ...",["exercise","watch TV","read books","meet friends"],0],["The speaker usually goes to bed at ...",["9:30 PM","10:00 PM","10:30 PM","11:00 PM"],2],["On weekends, the speaker enjoys ...",["staying at home","going shopping","visiting family","going to the movies"],2]]},{"t":"Texting and Walking","a":"l2-05.mp3","d":38,"w":"rjjpqrrn2luotxlp7potwqrvn3umqspsejlsqss2srjs3zltrktxlp2jxtphruun2ppqtqkp2wqmserzprsqrqtrpp2utut2","p":["Writing text messages and walking is dangerous. It is more dangerous than driving and texting. More people get injured while walking than driving. Walking in a straight line is not easy. We can forget how to walk properly. Dangerous things can happen. We run into people or cars. We fall over things in the street.","There are a few reasons why texting and walking is dangerous. People cannot see when they look at their keyboard. Their minds are somewhere else – they are not thinking about walking safely. Thousands of people have accidents. Some have serious head injuries."],"q":[["According to the text, texting and walking is ... texting and driving.",["less dangerous than","more dangerous than","as dangerous as","not as dangerous as"],1],["More people get ... while walking and texting than while driving.",["tickets","warnings","injured","stopped"],2],["When texting, people find it difficult to walk in a ...",["group","crowd","circle","straight line"],3],["When we text and walk, we can forget how to ...",["text properly","walk properly","talk properly","think properly"],1],["People who text while walking might run into ...",["problems","trouble","people or cars","walls"],2],["When texting, people cannot ... properly.",["see","hear","speak","touch"],0],["When people text, their minds are ...",["working harder","somewhere else","more focused","more alert"],1],["Some people who have accidents while texting and walking suffer from ...",["broken legs","head injuries","broken phones","small cuts"],1]]},{"t":"Our Green City","a":"l2-06.mp3","d":63,"w":"qsywpbpuorfvptnphoprdzssonqqf2jjpugsqn2ypnfsoykrosq2swt7srvtoshvpvussndzuzpximxtxqsnq3sqnzurvrxn","p":["Our city has started some new environmental programs this year. Now, all homes in the city can recycle. Before, only 30% of people recycled, but now 65% do. We have also planted 5,000 new trees in places that didn't have many trees. These trees help clean the air and make the city cooler in summer.","Our buses are getting better too. We have changed 20% of our buses to electric buses that don't make pollution. We have also built more bike lanes - now we have 75 kilometers instead of 30. A survey shows that 25% more people are using bikes now.","Our biggest project is putting solar panels on city buildings. This will reduce our use of normal electricity by about 15%. When we finish next year, these solar panels will give clean energy to about 10,000 homes."],"q":[["The percentage of people who recycled before the new programs was ...",["20%","30%","65%","75%"],1],["The percentage of people who recycle now is ...",["30%","45%","65%","75%"],2],["The number of new trees planted in the city is ...",["1,000","3,000","5,000","7,000"],2],["The new trees help make the city ... in summer.",["greener","cleaner","cooler","safer"],2],["The percentage of buses changed to electric buses is ...",["10%","20%","30%","40%"],1],["The length of bike lanes before was ... kilometers.",["20","30","50","75"],1],["According to the survey, the increase in people using bikes is ...",["15%","20%","25%","30%"],2],["The solar panels project will reduce normal electricity use by ...",["10%","15%","20%","25%"],1]]},{"t":"AI in Healthcare","a":"l2-07.mp3","d":70,"w":"tnntkmooqwqkzkilppqrpngtrnjkswp3zxmgzpstkm2sttnqoi2mslohmokrjphnhmqpotqk2kxlpsrtr2trsskosnwqsupf","p":["AI is changing healthcare in important ways. In medical imaging, AI can find possible problems in X-rays, MRIs, and CT scans. Some AI systems can find certain cancers with 94% accuracy - as good as experienced doctors but much faster.","AI helps find new medicines too. By looking at large databases of chemicals, AI programs can guess which ones might work against specific diseases. This has cut the first part of making new medicines from years to just months in some cases.","AI can also help make treatment plans just for you. AI can look at your genes, medical history, and lifestyle to suggest the best treatments. Early studies show these personal treatments can work up to 30% better.","But there are problems too. Many AI systems are like \"black boxes\" - they make decisions that even their creators don't fully understand. This raises questions about who is responsible when these systems are used for important healthcare decisions."],"q":[["AI can help find problems in ...",["blood tests","medical imaging","patient records","hospital systems"],1],["Some AI systems can find certain cancers with ... accuracy.",["84%","90%","94%","98%"],2],["Compared to doctors, AI systems work ...",["less accurately","more slowly","more accurately","much faster"],3],["AI helps in making new medicines by looking at ...",["patient symptoms","doctor reports","chemical databases","hospital records"],2],["The time to make new medicines has been reduced from years to ...",["weeks","months","days","hours"],1],["AI can make treatment plans based on your ...",["age","genes","height","weight"],1],["Personal treatments suggested by AI can work up to ... better.",["10%","20%","30%","40%"],2],["Many AI systems are described as \"black boxes\" because ...",["they are dark in color","they are small in size","they make decisions that are not fully understood","they are very expensive"],2]]},{"t":"Maggie's Trip","a":"l2-08.mp3","d":62,"w":"zrwtspqpnrjnrpvnwqruhstworltctuosmnoimjrqnnor2qsqimksq3usrpsqqrptultuvppurqsqtnvntmprrrqpqolrqps","p":["Hi Leroy, it's Maggie! I'm exhausted after a party last night where I stayed up until 3 AM.","Our trip has been amazing so far. Monday we spent in Edinburgh after a 10-hour coach ride with William. The people were friendly, though instead of trying Scottish food, we had hamburgers. I wanted a kilt but bought a castle photograph instead since kilts were too expensive.","Next, we took a train to Paris with Marcus, listening to his hip hop CDs. I skipped the French food and had a hot dog. Paris was beautiful and I bought postcards.","Then to Rome by coach with John. We had a great lunch at a motorway service station, and I bought a book souvenir in Rome.","Now I'm on a train near Bavaria with Elisabeth, who's playing the harmonica I bought her, rather poorly.","Tomorrow we're flying to Helsinki with Maria, who's nervous about flying. Our teacher said electronic goods are half-price there, so I'm planning to buy a computer!","Give mum and dad a kiss from me! Bye!"],"q":[["Maggie stayed up until ... at the party.",["1 AM","2 AM","3 AM","4 AM"],2],["In Edinburgh, Maggie and her friends traveled by ...",["train","plane","coach","car"],2],["Instead of Scottish food in Edinburgh, Maggie ate ...",["pizza","hamburgers","pasta","sandwiches"],1],["Maggie wanted to buy a ... in Edinburgh.",["kilt","photograph","postcard","book"],0],["Maggie traveled to Paris with ...",["William","Marcus","John","Elisabeth"],1],["In Rome, Maggie bought a ... as a souvenir.",["postcard","book","photo","harmonica"],1],["Maggie is currently near ...",["Paris","Rome","Bavaria","Helsinki"],2],["In Helsinki, Maggie plans to buy a ...",["camera","computer","phone","television"],1]]},{"t":"Camping Trip","a":"l2-09.mp3","d":55,"w":"uslmpolnwoskwnobqnowqrsgqpsqlrrrwqrsql2wtohnonrkdptvqpd2xptowxexwr2qtopvn3wrxrlvtbrrmqsegvrevzob","p":["Here are the instructions for next weekend's camping trip in Scotland. Arrive early to leave by 8am with our group of seventeen people. Check last week's email to confirm which of the two minibuses you should board.","We'll reach the camping area around 2pm, allowing time to buy food at the local supermarket before heading to our remote campsite. Four large tents will be set up, each with an experienced tent leader who went last year. Direct any problems to your designated tent leader.","Though we don't expect severe weather, Scottish hills are unpredictable, so bring wet weather clothing. If storms develop, we may need to relocate to town.","Cell phones likely won't work in the hills - please inform your parents of this. Phones will function in town, just not at the campsite.","For any additional questions, contact me or Mrs. Sanderson."],"q":[["The departure time for the camping trip is ...",["7 am","8 am","9 am","10 am"],1],["The total number of people going on the trip is ...",["7","11","17","27"],2],["The expected arrival time at the camping area is ...",["12 pm","2 pm","4 pm","6 pm"],1],["Before going to the campsite, the group will stop at a ...",["restaurant","supermarket","tourist office","gas station"],1],["The number of large tents for the trip is ...",["two","three","four","five"],2],["Each tent leader is someone who ...",["has special training","went last year","is a teacher","knows first aid"],1],["People should bring wet weather clothing because ...",["it will definitely rain","Scottish hills are cold","the weather is unpredictable","the tents might leak"],2],["Cell phones will ... at the campsite.",["work well","not work","work sometimes","need charging"],1]]},{"t":"Improving Local Parks","a":"l2-10.mp3","d":72,"w":"suxtvpvnssnuiuvvuukuhrhptwbvqtqsiqpmv9wssr9rowwkiqkuxm2urwsdnqpzzphqkopqqmuxxsvniunwtvoidyrrtoop","p":["Our city has started a new program to improve local parks. Over the past year, we've renovated eight parks and created three new ones. The largest project was Central Park, where we added new playgrounds, walking paths, and sports facilities.","The community response has been very positive. Visitor numbers have increased by 45% compared to last year. Families particularly appreciate the new play areas, which include equipment for children of different ages and abilities. The walking paths are popular with older residents, with 60% of seniors saying they now visit parks more frequently.","Local businesses near the parks have also benefited. Cafes and shops around Central Park report a 30% increase in weekend customers. The city plans to continue this initiative next year, focusing on five more parks in areas with limited green spaces.","Studies show that access to parks improves both physical and mental health. We hope these improvements will make our city a healthier and happier place for everyone."],"q":[["How many parks have been renovated?",["Three","Five","Eight","Ten"],2],["By what percentage have visitor numbers increased?",["25%","35%","45%","55%"],2],["The word \"renovated\" in the first paragraph is closest in meaning to:",["built","improved","purchased","inspected"],1],["What percentage of seniors visit parks more frequently now?",["40%","50%","60%","70%"],2],["Businesses near the parks have seen a weekend customer increase of:",["20%","30%","40%","50%"],1],["The word \"initiative\" in the third paragraph is closest in meaning to:",["problem","program","building","advertisement"],1],["How many new parks have been created?",["Two","Three","Four","Five"],1],["According to the text, access to parks improves:",["Only physical health","Only mental health","Both physical and mental health","Neither physical nor mental health"],2]]}],[{"t":"Digital Learning","a":"l3-01.mp3","d":77,"w":"wspnnqpoysrlsvqrpsswjxrspqunsonlxsrqsrjstsrmkhsvtequzvnmouvxppruqsnzrmvqnrooupwstpkumprqtkorznup","p":["Digital learning has changed education in many ways. Students today use computers, tablets, and online resources as part of their daily studies. According to a recent survey, 78% of schools now provide some form of digital learning tools.","One major benefit is personalized learning. Digital programs can adapt to each student's level and pace. Students who need more time on a topic can get it, while advanced students can move ahead faster. Research shows that this approach can improve learning outcomes by up to 20%.","Access to information is another advantage. Students can find resources from all over the world, not just from their textbooks. Virtual field trips allow classes to \"visit\" museums, historical sites, and even other countries without leaving the classroom.","However, there are challenges too. Not all students have equal access to technology at home. Schools are working to address this \"digital divide\" by providing devices and internet access to those who need it. Teachers also need training to use these tools effectively. Despite these challenges, digital learning continues to grow and evolve."],"q":[["What percentage of schools provide digital learning tools?",["58%","68%","78%","88%"],2],["By what percentage can personalized learning improve outcomes?",["10%","15%","20%","25%"],2],["The word \"adapt\" in the second paragraph is closest in meaning to:",["adjust","respond","resist","attach"],0],["What allows classes to \"visit\" museums without leaving the classroom?",["Field trips","Virtual field trips","Textbooks","Teacher presentations"],1],["What challenge related to digital learning is mentioned in the text?",["Cost of equipment","Student resistance","Unequal access to technology","Parental concerns"],2],["The word \"address\" in the last paragraph is closest in meaning to:",["locate","mail","speak to","tackle"],3],["According to the text, what do teachers need to use digital tools effectively?",["Better salaries","More time","Training","Newer equipment"],2],["The text suggests that digital learning is:",["A temporary trend","Too expensive for most schools","Continuing to grow and develop","Only suitable for advanced students"],2]]},{"t":"Healthy Eating","a":"l3-02.mp3","d":80,"w":"wstvvgtvsyxsvtqputrsursnztopmuwqqsstspqyydyoyrsvuvlvstpywusrttbxuvquqs2xsvpuqszousuuxotqqrstsstm","p":["Developing healthy eating habits is important for maintaining good health. Nutritionists recommend a balanced diet that includes a variety of fruits, vegetables, whole grains, and proteins. According to health experts, we should consume at least five servings of fruits and vegetables daily.","Portion control is another key aspect of healthy eating. Many people eat much larger portions than they need. Using smaller plates can help reduce portion sizes by 20% without leaving you feeling hungry. It's also important to eat slowly, as it takes about 20 minutes for your brain to register that you're full.","Meal planning can make healthy eating easier. Preparing meals in advance helps avoid unhealthy fast food options when you're busy or tired. Studies show that people who plan their meals are 40% more likely to maintain a healthy weight.","Staying hydrated is essential too. Experts recommend drinking 6-8 glasses of water daily. Sometimes thirst can be confused with hunger, so drinking water before meals can reduce unnecessary snacking. Making these small changes to your eating habits can lead to significant health benefits over time."],"q":[["How many servings of fruits and vegetables should we consume daily?",["Three","Four","Five","Six"],2],["Using smaller plates can reduce portion sizes by:",["10%","15%","20%","25%"],2],["The word \"maintaining\" in the first paragraph is closest in meaning to:",["achieving","improving","preserving","understanding"],2],["How long does it take for the brain to register fullness?",["5 minutes","10 minutes","15 minutes","20 minutes"],3],["People who plan their meals are what percentage more likely to maintain a healthy weight?",["20%","30%","40%","50%"],2],["The word \"essential\" in the last paragraph is closest in meaning to:",["optional","necessary","beneficial","recommended"],1],["Experts recommend drinking how many glasses of water daily?",["2-4 glasses","4-6 glasses","6-8 glasses","8-10 glasses"],2],["According to the text, thirst can sometimes be confused with:",["Dehydration","Hunger","Fatigue","Illness"],1]]},{"t":"Smart Homes","a":"l3-03.mp3","d":89,"w":"oqoqflpqptismllnqrrsqommrpsjlqrqp4nppqlssutsndztgyrsnvewsluplpnumxqlnqrgtumklrnmqju8qkxlmmrpqlok","p":["Smart home technology is becoming increasingly popular. These systems use internet-connected devices to monitor and control home functions like temperature, lighting, security, and entertainment. According to industry reports, about 35% of homes now have some type of smart technology.","One of the most common smart devices is the smart thermostat, which learns your preferences and adjusts heating and cooling automatically. These devices can reduce energy usage by 10-15% and save homeowners an average of $145 per year on utility bills.","Security is another popular application. Smart doorbells with cameras allow homeowners to see and speak to visitors remotely. Smart locks can be controlled from anywhere, and some even lock automatically when you leave home. Motion sensors and cameras provide additional security by alerting homeowners to unusual activity.","While convenience and security are the main benefits, there are concerns about privacy and data security. Manufacturers are working to address these issues with improved encryption and privacy controls. Despite these concerns, the smart home market continues to grow, with experts predicting that 60% of homes will have smart technology by 2025."],"q":[["What percentage of homes currently have smart technology?",["25%","35%","45%","55%"],1],["Smart thermostats can reduce energy usage by:",["5-10%","10-15%","15-20%","20-25%"],1],["The word \"popular\" in the first paragraph is closest in meaning to:",["expensive","complicated","widespread","new"],2],["How much can smart thermostats save homeowners annually?",["$95","$120","$145","$170"],2],["What allows homeowners to see and speak to visitors remotely?",["Smart locks","Motion sensors","Smart doorbells","Security cameras"],2],["The word \"concerns\" in the last paragraph is closest in meaning to:",["interests","worries","questions","discussions"],1],["What percentage of homes are predicted to have smart technology by 2025?",["40%","50%","60%","70%"],2],["According to the text, manufacturers are addressing privacy issues with:",["Lower prices","Improved encryption","Longer warranties","Simpler installation"],1]]},{"t":"A Complaint Call","a":"l3-04.mp3","d":68,"w":"ytsorlnspuojzsrxtbwqhrzkuupyfverrzosrmmtrunarlvptlwvussnnqswss4wtqqjqvvorowqoopm3tmunoovyq2urtqn","p":["Hello, my name is Harry Menzies, M-E-N-Z-I-E-S. I purchased a pool table from Excalibur Sports last week. I ordered it online Tuesday and it arrived just three days later - I was impressed with the delivery speed, but then the problems began.","First, the instruction booklet was only in Spanish and Polish - no English. Despite this, I managed to follow the diagrams and assemble it in about two hours.","When finished, I discovered the white cue ball was missing from the set. Fortunately, a friend lent me his cue ball so we could try the table, but we only played one game because we quickly realized the table was defective - the balls kept curving left or right instead of rolling straight.","I don't want to spend $800 on a defective pool table. My customer number is 45993 and the order number is GH12338. Please contact me at 348-28841 as soon as possible to resolve these issues. I'll also be writing a formal complaint to your head office."],"q":[["The correct spelling of the caller's surname is...",["Mensies","Menzies","Mensease","Mencies"],1],["The caller's experience with the delivery was...",["disappointingly slow","unexpectedly quick","complicated by rude staff","exactly as scheduled"],1],["A synonym for the word \"defective\"...",["expensive","damaged","faulty","complicated"],2],["The instruction booklet was provided in...",["French and German","Spanish and French","Polish and German","Spanish and Polish"],3],["The method used by the caller to assemble the table was...",["following verbal instructions","watching online videos","following the pictorial diagrams","hiring professional help"],2],["The specific problem with the pool table was related to...",["damaged table surface","abnormal ball movement","undersized pockets","unstable table legs"],1],["The phrase \"to resolve these issues\" could be substituted with...",["to explain these problems","to fix these matters","to understand these concerns","to ignore these difficulties"],1],["The caller's additional planned action beyond this call involves...",["requesting a partial refund","submitting a formal written complaint","posting an online review","visiting the store location"],1]]},{"t":"Sleep","a":"l3-05.mp3","d":80,"w":"tuwuvvv5zvwtquusvtov2wrquipvwsouwtnqtzpyquonywwhsxwwropxqvxhwrtxlsurjqqwhvqttukvvuqtrvumnrtzxoum","p":["Sleep is essential for our health and well-being, yet many people don't get enough quality rest. Adults generally need 7-9 hours of sleep per night, but studies show that about 35% of people regularly sleep less than 7 hours.","Sleep occurs in cycles consisting of different stages. Each complete cycle lasts approximately 90 minutes, and we typically experience 4-5 cycles per night. Deep sleep predominates in earlier cycles, while REM sleep increases in later cycles. This pattern is important for proper brain function.","One of sleep's crucial roles is memory consolidation. During deep sleep, the brain transfers information from short-term to long-term memory. Research shows that people who sleep after learning new information retain it 40% better than those who don't sleep.","Sleep deprivation has serious consequences. Just one night of poor sleep can impair attention and decision-making. Chronic sleep deprivation is linked to increased risk of heart disease, diabetes, and mental health problems. Despite these risks, many people still prioritize work and entertainment over sufficient sleep."],"q":[["The percentage of people who regularly sleep less than 7 hours per night is...",["25%","30%","35%","40%"],2],["The typical number of sleep cycles experienced per night is...",["2-3","4-5","6-7","8-9"],1],["A synonym for the word \"essential\" as used in the first paragraph would be...",["optional","necessary","helpful","recommended"],1],["People who sleep after learning retain information better by this percentage...",["20%","30%","40%","50%"],2],["The duration of a complete sleep cycle is...",["30 minutes","60 minutes","90 minutes","120 minutes"],2],["The word \"impair\" in the last paragraph could be replaced with...",["improve","damage","maintain","measure"],1],["The sleep stage that becomes more prominent in later cycles of the night is...",["Light sleep","Deep sleep","REM sleep","Transition sleep"],2],["According to the text, chronic sleep deprivation is associated with...",["Improved memory","Better decision-making","Heart disease","Increased energy"],2]]},{"t":"Queen Hatshepsut","a":"l3-06.mp3","d":68,"w":"vutsvtuupsrrnorrskpqxtpruqqsysooqtvqyqsntnvuolxtquuqsqqmmputtuutrrmwuoztpmyrwsusnqsuvosvsovppopl","p":["Egyptian archaeologists announced on Wednesday that they have identified a mummy discovered in 1903 as that of Queen Hatshepsut, Egypt's most powerful female pharaoh.","Queen Hatshepsut was the only woman to rule ancient Egypt while the kingdom was at the height of its wealth and power, from about 1502 to 1482 B.C. Of all the female pharaohs–including Cleopatra and Nefertiti – Hatshepsut's reign was the longest and most successful. While in power, she established trade routes and built hundreds of monuments and temples throughout Egypt. Despite her prosperous reign, both her mummy and her legacy were virtually erased from Egyptian history. Many historians believe that Tuthmose III, Hatshepsut's stepson, destroyed records and monuments bearing her name. It may have been his revenge. It is believed that she stole the throne from him. Finding the mummy of this powerful queen may provide details about an important part of Egyptian history."],"q":[["The year when the mummy that was later identified as Queen Hatshepsut was discovered is...",["1482 B.C.","1502 B.C.","1903","2023"],2],["Queen Hatshepsut's position in Egyptian history can be described as...",["the most beautiful female pharaoh","the most powerful female pharaoh","the youngest female pharaoh","the last female pharaoh"],1],["The approximate duration of Queen Hatshepsut's rule was...",["10 years","15 years","20 years","25 years"],2],["During her reign, Queen Hatshepsut's achievements included...",["conquering neighboring countries","building monuments and establishing trade","creating a new writing system","making peace with all enemies"],1],["The relationship between Tuthmose III and Hatshepsut was that of...",["husband and wife","brother and sister","stepson and stepmother","cousin and cousin"],2],["The word \"virtually\" in the phrase \"virtually erased from Egyptian history\" could be replaced with...",["partially","almost completely","temporarily","accidentally"],1],["According to the text, Tuthmose III's possible motivation for destroying records of Hatshepsut was...",["religious beliefs","political strategy","revenge","cultural traditions"],2],["The discovery of Queen Hatshepsut's mummy is significant because it might...",["prove she was murdered","reveal details about an important historical period","show she was related to Cleopatra","demonstrate her physical appearance"],1]]},{"t":"The Circus","a":"l3-07.mp3","d":97,"w":"qytoioixpjuttsrlrgq3xoxqsgwkxurznxwrpqrsnvumwnzulrqtmtolrluploummjnwqkwkossjvqrttoqcppqsnugosrjp","p":["Every year, the Rodriguez Brothers Circus arrives and stays for a week. Then they go to the next town.","There are not many animals in the circus. People told the circus that they didn't like seeing animals performing. There is an elephant called Jacob and two old lions, Hattie and Meg.","Most of the performers are human! There is Leopold, The Strongest Man In The World. His father also worked in the circus, but Leopold is stronger than him, he has bigger arms and bigger legs too! Leopold performs his act every night for the town's people who come to watch.","Another performer is Clara. She says she has the longest hair in the world. It's about 4 metres long! She also has a daughter who works in the circus. Her name is Sue-Ellen. Her hair is a lot shorter, but she wants to grow it as long as her mother's. Sue-Ellen helps look after the animals and she's also learning how to juggle.","The highlight of the circus are the three clowns, Pit, Pot and Pat. They all wear long red shoes, but Pat's shoes are the longest and sometimes, he falls over because they're so long! They perform for about twenty minutes and they are always the most popular act with the audience, especially the children. Many people think Pit, Pot and Pat are three brothers, but Pat is older than the other two - he's their father! He's the oldest clown in the country, but he has a lot of energy."],"q":[["The Rodriguez Brothers Circus stays in each town for ...",["a day","a week","a month","two weeks"],1],["The circus has ... animals performing.",["no","many","not many","only exotic"],2],["The name of the circus elephant is ...",["Hattie","Meg","Jacob","Leopold"],2],["Leopold is stronger than ...",["his brother","his father","his son","his grandfather"],1],["Clara's hair is approximately ... long.",["2 metres","3 metres","4 metres","5 metres"],2],["Sue-Ellen is learning how to ...",["dance","juggle","sing","ride elephants"],1],["The most popular act in the circus is ...",["the elephant","the lions","the clowns","the strongest man"],2],["Pat is ... to Pit and Pot.",["a brother","a cousin","a friend","a father"],3]]},{"t":"Learning a Language","a":"l3-08.mp3","d":50,"w":"vtoqphqtoqmmp5xotrmhls2nqvnjnu3mpnxkmuts3wczrwwuqtnmwpp4npmmoquqqvktoomqqg2qtunmarnptrihorppqpkg","p":["Learning a new language can be a challenging experience. However, this does not have to be the case for you. There are a few tips you can follow to make learning easy and fun.","The first step to learn more efficiently is to have a goal. It is better to have several small goals that you can achieve over a short period. For example, you can purpose to learn all vocabulary related to traveling one week, and cooking vocabulary the next.","Another tip is to study smart. Learning a language requires a lot of memorizing, so be sure to employ the right techniques. Flashcards can come in real handy in this respect.","Finally, make sure to study the language every day. Consistent practice is the best way to remember everything you learn."],"q":[["Learning a new language is described as a ... experience.",["fun","challenging","boring","quick"],1],["The text suggests that learning a new language can be ...",["impossible","only for young people","easy and fun","only difficult"],2],["For efficient learning, it is better to have ... goals.",["one big","several small","no specific","only long-term"],1],["An example of a small goal is learning vocabulary related to ...",["grammar","traveling","reading","writing"],1],["Learning a language requires a lot of ...",["money","friends","memorizing","teachers"],2],["A helpful tool mentioned for memorizing vocabulary is ...",["books","videos","flashcards","music"],2],["The best way to remember everything you learn is ...",["studying once a week","consistent practice","watching movies","speaking with natives"],1],["The text recommends studying the language ...",["once a week","twice a week","every weekend","every day"],3]]},{"t":"Christmas","a":"l3-09.mp3","d":50,"w":"vymmrvkn8rrluqxrbtzssprklvmsxognwzqqtsr5qrqqnmwiopwvupdgsmqosltg2oosfmvwudqpbqzpf2tryu8uuo2xqsrd","p":["I enjoy many holidays, but my favorite holiday is Christmas. Many of my happiest childhood memories revolve around Christmas, and I love making new memories with my children today.","We start the Christmas season by putting up our Christmas tree. A beautiful star from my grandmother hangs on the very top. We decorate our house with lights, wreaths, and greenery.","Gift giving is an important part of our Christmas traditions. This year, I am painting portraits for my parents and grandparents. My son's Christmas list includes a remote control car, a basketball hoop, and new clothes for school.","The only thing I do not love about Christmas is the cold weather! I would rather have warmth and sunshine."],"q":[["The speaker's favorite holiday is ...",["Easter","Christmas","Thanksgiving","New Year"],1],["The speaker enjoys making new Christmas memories with ...",["friends","parents","siblings","children"],3],["The Christmas season starts with putting up ...",["lights","decorations","a Christmas tree","wreaths"],2],["On top of their Christmas tree hangs a ...",["star","angel","bell","ribbon"],0],["The star on the tree originally belonged to ...",["the speaker","the speaker's mother","the speaker's grandmother","the speaker's child"],2],["This year, the speaker is giving ... to parents and grandparents.",["money","clothes","books","portraits"],3],["The speaker's son wants a ... for Christmas.",["video game","remote control car","bicycle","new phone"],1],["The one thing the speaker does not like about Christmas is ...",["shopping","cooking","cold weather","family gatherings"],2]]},{"t":"Janet's Routine","a":"l3-10.mp3","d":94,"w":"smsuskrsqpkrrrtrtpnurltmwpptlvsnoststpqoppurrplqtrnnsrfrpumpuvpjrstjunrntszpmssruskrlpssromwrroj","p":["Janet is an athlete, and she wakes up at 4:30 am every weekday morning. She spends the first 30 minutes reading and then 15 minutes meditating. At 5:15 am Janet checks her email for only 30 minutes and then goes for her first run of the day. She runs for an hour and a half along the lake near her house. After running, Janet has a shower and then prepares breakfast, which is usually cereal and fruit. However, she occasionally has a less healthy breakfast.","She usually finishes breakfast at around 8 am. If it is a weekday, she always leaves the house at 8:20 and goes to training. Her training starts at 9 am, and she needs 30 minutes to drive to the gym. She trains for 3 hours with her team and then goes home for lunch. She always eats a very big and healthy lunch. As soon as she finishes lunch, she has a nap for one hour.","After her nap, she likes to go for a walk around the lake and look at nature. She sometimes reads or meditates at the lake in the afternoon. In the evening, during the week, she meets up with friends. Most of her friends are athletes too, so they have a lot to talk about.","She typically goes to bed at 9 pm because she prefers to be awake in the morning than at night. She sometimes falls asleep listening to music, but she never watches the television or reads anything on her tablet. She always makes sure her alarm is set and is almost always asleep by 9:45 pm."],"q":[["Janet wakes up at ... every weekday morning.",["4:00 am","4:30 am","5:00 am","5:30 am"],1],["After waking up, Janet first spends time ...",["running","meditating","reading","checking email"],2],["Janet's morning run lasts for ...",["30 minutes","45 minutes","one hour","one hour and a half"],3],["For breakfast, Janet usually eats ...",["eggs and toast","cereal and fruit","yogurt and granola","a sandwich"],1],["Janet needs ... minutes to drive to the gym.",["20","25","30","40"],2],["After lunch, Janet always ...",["goes for a walk","reads a book","takes a nap","meets friends"],2],["In the evenings during weekdays, Janet ...",["watches TV","studies","meets friends","goes running again"],2],["Janet usually goes to bed at ...",["8:30 pm","9:00 pm","9:30 pm","10:00 pm"],1]]}],[{"t":"Memories of My Father","a":"l4-01.mp3","d":110,"w":"wssttrpnroxnzrrrqpuupjtstuuosnqttpwrkuwuqunyujvtrurupuvrsssrsntnqvnwrlstmvvrvpqmvtotpqrrrtypsurq","p":["Good evening. I'm Jessica Talbot, and I'd like to share some personal memories of my father on this tenth anniversary of his passing. We've heard from many industry professionals tonight about his remarkable talent, but my mother and I hope to give you a glimpse of the man behind the camera – the father and husband. Though he never won an Oscar for fatherhood, to me, it was his greatest achievement.","My earliest memory, when I was about three or four, was sailing with him on his beloved yacht in the Pacific. Two things fascinated me: the vastness of the ocean and my father's incredibly hairy chest visible through his unbuttoned navy shirt. On later trips, he would tell me the world was mine if I wanted it – that the only limit was my ambition. I owe my self-belief to these early encouragements.","That yacht was his escape from Hollywood's chaos. He was happiest there – sometimes with my mother, sometimes just the two of us – with sunlight dancing on waves and wind in our hair. When I picture him truly relaxed, it's on that boat, not at home where scripts and phone calls constantly demanded his attention. Even today, I feel closest to him when I'm at sea.","Few people know about his passion for painting. Next year, we hope to publish a collection of his work. He particularly loved capturing the coastline north of Los Angeles, often spending hours perfecting the tufts of grass on sand dunes. I remember once, at age five or six, he told me \"we have to wait for the light.\" I was confused until the sun emerged from behind clouds, and he flashed that famous smile – the smile millions adored on screen, but I was lucky enough to see every day, just for me. That was my dad."],"q":[["The occasion for Jessica Talbot's speech is...",["a memorial service","a decade since her father's death","a film industry award ceremony","a book launch event"],1],["Jessica describes her father's greatest accomplishment as...",["his cinematic achievements","his parenting role","his sailing expertise","his artistic pursuits"],1],["The speaker's earliest recollection of her father involves...",["traveling on his watercraft","attending a film premiere","visiting his workplace","exploring a foreign country"],0],["According to Jessica, her father instilled in her a sense of...",["humility","caution","confidence","discipline"],2],["The place where Jessica's father found true contentment was...",["on movie sets","at their residence","on his vessel","in his studio"],2],["An artistic pursuit of Jessica's father that remained largely unknown was...",["composing music","creating visual art","writing poetry","sculpting"],1],["Jessica's family intends to showcase her father's artwork...",["at a gallery exhibition","in a printed collection","at a charity auction","on a dedicated website"],1],["The natural subject her father particularly enjoyed depicting in his art was...",["mountain ranges","wildlife scenes","urban landscapes","shoreline scenery"],3]]},{"t":"The Solar System","a":"l4-02.mp3","d":55,"w":"unpoko9mzxo4qnwmvoroh8rpqrnnvnrlftoeomsk6wopn7wopshqxo6tnrtrorlk2thtrkns2vrvoorpv4nrq89phxrjsjpe","p":["Our solar system has one star and eight planets. There are several other representatives, such as the tiny heavenly bodies that rotate around the planets known as the satellites. The sun is a dazzling, gaseous star, and it is a million times greater than Earth. Sun gives light, heat, and energy in our solar system.","There are eight planets on the solar system. While Mercury is the closest to the sun, Neptune is farthest away. Jupiter and Venus are the 'largest' and 'brightest' of all planets, respectively. Mars is known as a 'cruel planet' in Indian mythology.","All the planets revolve around the sun in elliptical paths known as orbits. They are spinning on their axis. Except for Venus, all other planets are continually moving counterclockwise."],"q":[["The solar system contains ... in addition to planets.",["comets","satellites","moons","asteroids"],1],["The sun is described as a ... star.",["luminous","fiery","radiant","gaseous"],3],["Compared to Earth, the sun is ...",["a thousand times larger","ten thousand times greater","a million times bigger","billions of times more massive"],2],["The planet positioned nearest to the sun is ...",["Venus","Earth","Mercury","Mars"],2],["Jupiter is notable for being the ... planet.",["most massive","most distant","most brilliant","most colorful"],0],["In Indian mythology, ... is referred to as a \"cruel planet.\"",["Saturn","Mars","Mercury","Neptune"],1],["The path of planetary movement around the sun is ...",["circular","oval","elliptical","spiral"],2],["Most planets in our solar system rotate in a ... direction.",["clockwise","counterclockwise","random","variable"],1]]},{"t":"The Maine Coon","a":"l4-03.mp3","d":50,"w":"vnnoo2tovr7tsovssmmwmsmspqhxhlpfrenhjtyeqsnr3mnqqotrssm2yiturb2tzqrnqlnlmkn2lvtwmopunsoj2suspjvb","p":["The largest of the domesticated breeds of cats, the Maine Coon originated in Maine. It is well adapted to the harsh climate of the north with thick, shaggy fur.","In the 1800s, since they are excellent hunters, they were farm cats, barn cats and often, ship's cats. No one knows their true origin, but some say they came with the Vikings. Other stories say that their","ancestors were sent over from France by Marie Antoinette. She, of course, never survived to make the voyage.","The coon part of their name may come from Captain Charles Coon, a ship's captain, who had cats like these. They are often called the \"dogs of the cat world\" because they are so people friendly and laid back. They are now one of the most popular cat breeds in the US."],"q":[["The Maine Coon is characterized as the ... of domesticated cat breeds.",["most ancient","most substantial","most aggressive","most intelligent"],1],["Maine Coons have developed ... that helps them survive in northern climates.",["dense, rough coat","unique coloration","specialized paws","distinctive facial features"],0],["In the 19th century, Maine Coons were primarily utilized as ...",["exhibition animals","royal companions","household mousers","agricultural predators"],3],["One theory suggests Maine Coons arrived with ...",["Pilgrims","Nordic explorers","Spanish sailors","English settlers"],1],["Another origin story connects the breed to ...",["a French monarch","Italian nobility","Russian traders","German immigrants"],0],["The \"coon\" portion of their name potentially derives from ...",["a nautical commander","their striped appearance","their hunting behavior","a geographical region"],0],["Maine Coons are frequently compared to canines due to their ...",["territorial nature","noisy communication","sociable temperament","physical stamina"],2],["In the United States today, Maine Coons are ...",["rare and endangered","highly sought after","difficult to domesticate","primarily working animals"],1]]},{"t":"Collecting Stamps","a":"l4-04.mp3","d":50,"w":"rtlsooo2xrptnkqwtpuosjtopxrnmqh2ttjooenzkxxszdatps6ytvunsy2prkulsn2ovq7zfuspp2nsxovisyr5juvqosoc","p":["Collecting postage stamps remains a relaxing hobby for many. While some collections consist of millions of stamps, it is more common for a collection to specialize on a single subject.","For example, one might only collect stamps that feature famous athletes. Others gather stamps from a specific country. Whatever the focus, it is still important to have the proper equipment.","Invest in a good stamp album. It should have plenty of room to add pages as your collection grows.","Fasten stamps to each page with specialized hinges. Hinges are necessary; otherwise, you might damage the backs of your stamps. One can also store them in a special stock book, which do not require hinges. These supplies are found in special stores that cater to collectors."],"q":[["Stamp collecting is described as a ... activity.",["profitable","soothing","challenging","disappearing"],1],["Most collections tend to ...",["cover numerous themes","focus on a particular topic","include rare examples only","emphasize monetary value"],1],["One possible specialization mentioned in the text is collecting stamps featuring ...",["celebrated sports figures","endangered wildlife","architectural landmarks","historical events"],0],["For a successful collection, the text emphasizes the importance of ...",["joining collector clubs","appropriate materials","frequent trading","professional evaluation"],1],["A stamp album should provide ...",["protective covering","decorative elements","space for expansion","alphabetical organization"],2],["The purpose of stamp hinges is to ...",["increase value","prevent deterioration","enhance appearance","identify authenticity"],1],["An alternative to using hinges for storage is ...",["transparent envelopes","specialized portfolios","designated stock books","archival containers"],2],["Equipment for stamp collecting can be purchased at ...",["ordinary stationery shops","specialty establishments","online auctions only","government offices"],1]]},{"t":"Marina and Erin","a":"l4-05.mp3","d":86,"w":"zuruoutspttspunrvuwcruqrsutqr9wsrrtwmvwpuuv8xtpxostqssprlususqrxtsrovpuorrrqvsuqsqmurrxotxrspqmp","p":["Marina and Erin are best friends, but they have very different interests. Marina is a very artistic person. In her free time, she likes to do things that keep her mind active and that make her think. She enjoys reading books, and usually reads one book per week. She listens to music, too. One of her favorite things to do is to write in her journal the song lyrics that inspire her most. Marina is learning how to play the guitar, and she hopes to one day write her own music. When Marina goes out on the weekends, she likes to go to the movie theater to watch popular films. She is a major film buff, and she enjoys watching a lot of classic movies, too.","Erin is much more athletic than Marina and likes to stay physically active. She plays both soccer and basketball through school, and she loves to dance. During the summer, her favorite activity is swimming in her backyard pool. For as much as she is active, Erin also likes to eat and cook a lot of food. She frequently goes shopping in the supermarket and walks around the aisles to find the perfect ingredients for her next dish. Erin often invites Marina to her house to try her newest recipes. The two get along very well with each other, and they both think that their interests complement each other's personality."],"q":[["Marina could best be described as someone who is ...",["scholarly","creative","reserved","practical"],1],["Marina's reading habit involves completing approximately ... books monthly.",["two","three","four","five"],2],["When documenting music in her journal, Marina focuses on ...",["melody compositions","performer biographies","meaningful verses","critical reviews"],2],["Marina's musical aspiration is to eventually ...",["perform professionally","compose original pieces","teach instrumental skills","record famous songs"],1],["In contrast to Marina, Erin prioritizes ...",["intellectual pursuits","physical activities","social gatherings","artistic expression"],1],["During summer months, Erin particularly enjoys ...",["aquatic recreation","outdoor hiking","camping expeditions","beach volleyball"],0],["Erin's culinary interest involves ...",["ordering takeout meals","studying nutrition","selecting choice components","following family recipes"],2],["The friendship between Marina and Erin works well because their interests ...",["are identical","are harmonious","occasionally overlap","constantly compete"],1]]},{"t":"Making Plans","a":"l4-06.mp3","d":75,"w":"uotohqrqpinntsppimljqhooqkkpoknoprqlkspkoum2vslunhnp8owossgzuqxqqlqhmogwjpwmkoqvqekmrksmrlpuvqln","p":["After work, school, and home obligations are completed, as well as during any other spare time one might have, most individuals make plans, or arrangements to engage in a particular activity at a particular time—usually with others.","Plans can be comprised of a wide variety of activities, but most of the time they concern social engagements. Thus, one can make plans to go to the movies or visit a museum, but it's unlikely that anyone will make plans to clean the house or visit the doctor.","Furthermore, typical plans vary from person to person, and from age group to age group. Teenagers will probably make plans to go to the movies or visit the mall; young adults will probably make plans to visit a bar/club or some sort of show; and adults and elderly individuals will probably make plans to partake in all the mentioned activities and then some, with a special focus on relaxing and having fun. In short, plans are leisure and recreation-related arrangements to engage in personally enjoyable hobbies or activities, usually with others."],"q":[["According to the text, individuals typically make arrangements for activities during their ...",["mandatory hours","leisure time","working periods","educational sessions"],1],["The text indicates that plans are predominantly related to ...",["professional obligations","domestic responsibilities","interpersonal engagements","health maintenance"],2],["An example of what would not normally be considered \"making plans\" is ...",["arranging a cinema outing","scheduling household chores","organizing a museum visit","coordinating a social gathering"],1],["The text suggests that planning preferences are influenced by ...",["geographical location","economic status","generational differences","educational background"],2],["Adolescents are described as typically arranging ...",["cultural exhibitions","commercial venue visits","educational activities","family gatherings"],1],["Young adults' arrangements commonly involve ...",["recreational facilities","entertainment establishments","outdoor expeditions","educational seminars"],1],["The text portrays mature and elderly individuals as ...",["having limited planning options","preferring solitary activities","seeking diverse recreational pursuits","avoiding social engagements"],2],["The fundamental essence of \"plans\" as described in the text is ...",["mandatory social obligations","professional development activities","enjoyable personal arrangements","necessary life responsibilities"],2]]},{"t":"Blake in New York","a":"l4-07.mp3","d":68,"w":"txusziwuprttppmszruvsxqxoqvppsrrrnmtwssnzpt2xurssvqtsrwsqoopnjtmvvts4tsvvussuqvstnwuooovttrptwpl","p":["The airplane Blake is on is about ready to land in New York. His aunt and grandmother, on the seats next to him, gather their bags in preparation to get off the plane. They plan to visit Ellis Island, where his great-grandfather's name, Rocko Garoni, is inscribed on the Wall of Honor. Rocko immigrated to the United States from Italy in 1920. He was 16 and came across the ocean all alone to try to find a better life and earn money to send back to his family.","The plane trip took five hours, because they flew from Utah to New York. It wasn't too bad because he could read, watch TV or listen to music in his seat, but it was nice to get up to stretch his legs.","As they got off the plane, Blake and his aunt talked about what they should do first. They needed to check into their hotel room, of course, but after that they wanted to visit the Statue of Liberty and maybe try to climb the stairs up to the crown. It was 354 steps, but he wanted to try to do it and his aunt said she would try as well. They planned to go to Ellis Island the next morning."],"q":[["The aircraft carrying Blake is preparing to arrive in ...",["New Jersey","New York","Pennsylvania","Massachusetts"],1],["The individuals accompanying Blake on this journey are his ...",["mother and grandfather","relative and elderly relation","uncle and great-grandmother","cousin and maternal grandparent"],1],["The reason for their visit to Ellis Island is to view a ...",["commemorative inscription","historical photograph","family document","museum exhibition"],0],["Blake's ancestor Rocko departed from ... in 1920.",["Spain","Greece","Italy","Portugal"],2],["The duration of their air travel was extended because they were flying from ...",["the western region","the southern states","across the continent","a distant territory"],0],["During the flight, Blake occupied himself by ...",["conversing with fellow passengers","completing academic assignments","engaging with various media","planning their itinerary"],2],["Following hotel registration, their intended destination was ...",["a renowned monument","a historical museum","a famous bridge","a cultural center"],0],["The ascent they were considering involved approximately ... stairs.",["two hundred and fifty","three hundred and fifty","four hundred and fifty","five hundred and fifty"],1]]},{"t":"Stereotyping","a":"l4-08.mp3","d":65,"w":"qpnrsfsrpdtdoq8tnppqrpvdpngjtu6osqrllqppqknsstlqq5rmsissuslzhsrum2w7swnqvmpnrlhsmpjps4rrlipqrsog","p":["America has been known for creating stereotypes throughout history- from 'Baby Boomers' to 'flappers' and even more. What is stereotyping? Stereotyping is when you make assumptions about an individual or group based on the whole race, religion, culture, ethnicity, or other quality. It is important to note that while some stereotypes can be positive, a majority of pre-existing ones are negative.","Many of us fall prey to stereotyping others without even realizing it. However, in today's modern world, the younger generation has developed a more unique, and broader mindset that may foretell the end of stereotyping. How do they do it? One easy tip to follow is to always keep an open-mindset and accept that everyone around you is different; no two individuals are forced to behave the same based on their shared qualities. By being open and accepting, we are taking one step closer to a world of freedom and diversity."],"q":[["The text identifies America as a country that has ...",["eliminated prejudice","generated categorizations","celebrated diversity","rejected classifications"],1],["The term \"stereotyping\" is defined in the text as making ... about individuals.",["generalizations","compliments","inquiries","connections"],0],["According to the passage, the nature of most existing stereotypes tends to be ...",["beneficial","unfavorable","neutral","evolving"],1],["The text suggests that many people engage in stereotyping ...",["deliberately","occasionally","unconsciously","reluctantly"],2],["The contemporary generation is characterized as having a ... perspective.",["conventional","restricted","expansive","traditional"],2],["The passage indicates that the younger generation's attitude may lead to ...",["strengthening stereotypes","perpetuating biases","dissolving preconceptions","creating new categories"],2],["A recommended approach for countering stereotypes includes maintaining ...",["social distance","receptive thinking","critical judgment","established beliefs"],1],["By embracing acceptance, the text suggests we are progressing toward a world of ...",["uniformity and structure","tradition and stability","order and consistency","liberty and variation"],3]]},{"t":"The History of Chocolate","a":"l4-09.mp3","d":59,"w":"qrsqodksqqpl4rlrlrrhqrqsu4srrplfnosdotntotpptlomvqpq8wrsuoptdrkiyqtqss9uwzluqqvhvqnnkmwjuhsrmupn","p":["Chocolate has always been one of America's favorite treats, but did you know that Americans were not the first to consume it? We can thank ancient civilizations such as the Olmecs and Mayans for the discovery and invention of cocoa bean products.","Cocoa beans are derived from fruits of cacao trees, plants native to Central and South America. It is commonly thought that the Olmec civilization used cocoa beans in the creation of ceremonial drinks, however, there has not been sufficient evidence to prove that.","This knowledge of chocolate was passed to the Mayans who not only used but revered it. Historical evidence has shown that chocolate was used in grand celebrations and to finalize important transactions in Mayan culture. Over its life span, chocolate has been used as a stimulant, treat, form of currency, rite of passage, and now delicacy that we all know and love."],"q":[["The text indicates that Americans were ...",["pioneers in chocolate production","subsequent consumers of chocolate","inventors of chocolate processing","primary exporters of chocolate"],1],["The discovery of cocoa bean products is attributed to ...",["European explorers","ancient Mesoamerican societies","South American traders","medieval alchemists"],1],["Cacao trees are indigenous to ...",["African regions","Asian territories","Latin American areas","Mediterranean zones"],2],["Regarding the Olmec civilization, the text suggests their chocolate use is ...",["thoroughly documented","historically verified","essentially theoretical","recently discovered"],2],["In Mayan culture, chocolate was utilized for ...",["everyday nourishment","significant occasions","medicinal purposes","agricultural rituals"],1],["Historical evidence indicates that Mayans used chocolate to ...",["preserve food supplies","conclude important deals","communicate with deities","treat common ailments"],1],["Throughout history, chocolate has functioned as ...",["exclusively a luxury item","primarily a medicinal substance","mainly a religious symbol","multiple different roles"],3],["The text describes modern chocolate as a ...",["common commodity","refined indulgence","nutritional necessity","therapeutic substance"],1]]},{"t":"Thomas Edison","a":"l4-10.mp3","d":91,"w":"uvqvxsspjqutpwtrkwvwoqtvtsrwrsusrouuykqqrrtrsovzqsqqrwurwvrkupsruqlxwqvrovtvutuqpyxsuturuwutssuk","p":["Possibly the most famous American inventor, Thomas Edison set a world record by holding 1093 patents. Born in 1847 in Ohio, he was the youngest of seven children, only four of whom survived to become adults. In early childhood, Thomas developed hearing loss, which became permanent. His hearing difficulties later influenced his work as an inventor.","He had very little formal schooling because his deafness interfered. But he read everything he could get his hands on. He dropped out of school, going to work for the railroad. He became fascinated by the telegraph, which at the time transmitted by marking dots and dashes on paper. When telegraphers had to be able to hear, he turned to improving the machines and was able to add a printer. He then became an inventor full time, eventually setting up a laboratory in Menlo Park, New Jersey.","Because he was self-educated, Edison approached experimentation differently than did scientists. He used the trial and error method. If one thing didn't work, he tried another. In this manner he first invented the phonograph, which evolved into the record player, and the incandescent light bulb. The light bulb led to the development of electric lighting throughout the world.","Edison was brilliant, but a difficult man to work with and often neglected his family. He died in 1931 of diabetes."],"q":[["Thomas Edison's noteworthy achievement was holding ... patents.",["just over a thousand","slightly under a thousand","several hundred","a few dozen"],0],["Edison's position in his family structure was ...",["the eldest offspring","a middle child","the final sibling","an only child"],2],["A medical condition that affected Edison throughout his life was ...",["visual impairment","auditory limitation","respiratory difficulty","mobility restriction"],1],["Edison's formal education was ...",["extensive and thorough","focused on sciences","minimal and brief","completed at university"],2],["Before becoming a full-time inventor, Edison was employed in the ... industry.",["transportation","publishing","medical","educational"],0],["Edison's experimental methodology primarily involved ...",["theoretical analysis","systematic testing","mathematical calculation","collaborative research"],1],["The invention that eventually evolved into modern sound reproduction systems was the ...",["telegraph","phonograph","dynamo","kinetoscope"],1],["The text characterizes Edison's personality as ...",["gentle and accommodating","reserved and methodical","challenging and neglectful","sociable and family-oriented"],2]]}]],"read":[[{"t":"Where Is My Phone?","n":125,"p":["I cannot find my phone. Where is my phone? My phone was in my hand. Now my phone is gone. I lose my phone sometimes. When I lose my phone, I feel angry.","Did I leave it at work, did I leave it at school, or did I leave it in my car? My phone is black. My phone is shaped like a rectangle. When my phone rings, it sounds like a bird. My phone is not ringing right now.","I am looking for my phone. I am looking on the table and under the table. I am looking in the kitchen. I am looking in the bathroom, the bedroom, and the basement. I cannot find my phone. Oh! My phone is in my pocket."],"q":[["The author cannot find their...",["key","phone","wallet","tablet"],1],["The phone is... in color.",["white","blue","black","red"],2],["When the author loses their phone, they feel...",["happy","sad","angry","tired"],2],["At the beginning of the story, the phone was...",["in the author's hand","on the table","in the kitchen","in the car"],0],["The shape of the phone is like a...",["square","circle","triangle","rectangle"],3],["When the phone rings, it sounds like a...",["dog","bird","song","bell"],1],["The author looks for the phone...",["only in the bedroom","only in the kitchen","only in the car","in many different places"],3],["At the end of the story, the author finds the phone...",["at work","in the car","in their pocket","under the table"],2]]},{"t":"Stamp Collecting","n":297,"p":["Hello, my name is Mark. I have a hobby. I collect stamps. Every day, I look at my stamp collection. I have stamps from many countries. I have stamps with animals, flowers, and famous people. I have over 500 stamps.","On weekends, I go to the stamp market. I meet other collectors there. We talk about stamps and sometimes trade them. I also visit the post office. They have new stamps every month. I buy them and add them to my collection.","My favourite stamp is one with a big red dragon. It is from China. I like it because it is colourful and unique. Collecting stamps teaches me about different places. I learn about history and culture. I enjoy my hobby very much. It makes me happy.","Sometimes, I write letters to friends in other countries. They send me stamps from their letters. I keep the stamps and read their letters. I also join a stamp club at school. We meet every Thursday. We show our new stamps and talk about them.","My family supports my hobby. My father gives me his old stamps. My mother helps me organise my collection. I use special books to keep the stamps safe. I am careful when I handle them. I clean my stamps gently.","I also read magazines about stamp collecting. I learn how to find rare stamps. I dream about finding a very old stamp one day. Collecting stamps is fun and interesting. It is a simple hobby, but it brings me joy. I hope to collect more stamps every year.","When I look at my stamps, I feel proud. Each stamp has a story. They show me the world on small pieces of paper. I love my hobby, and I want to share it with others."],"q":[["... collects stamps",["John","Mark","Peter","David"],1],["The person has more than... stamps in their collection.",["100","300","500","1000"],2],["On weekends, the collector goes to the...",["post office","library","stamp market","school"],2],["The collector's favourite stamp has a...",["blue butterfly","red dragon","yellow flower","green tree"],1],["The favourite stamp comes from...",["Japan","Russia","America","China"],3],["The stamp club at school meets every...",["Monday","Wednesday","Thursday","Friday"],2],["The collector's mother helps to... the collection.",["organise","clean","buy","sell"],0],["To keep stamps safe, the collector uses...",["envelopes","special books","boxes","folders"],1]]},{"t":"Tim's Day","n":227,"p":["Every day Tim wakes up at five thirty when his alarm clock rings. He gets up and then goes to the bathroom and has a long, hot shower. After that he makes breakfast for him and his wife, Betty. Tim has coffee and two slices of toast and Betty drinks a cup of tea and eats a bowl of cornflakes. At six o'clock Tim brushes his teeth, always before he gets dressed because it is very important that he doesn't get toothpaste on his clothes - Tim is a train driver and he wears a uniform! Finally, he kisses his wife and baby son and leaves his house in Watford at a quarter after six.","Tim starts work at seven o'clock and drives trains on the London Underground. He usually works in the mornings from Monday to Friday, but he sometimes works on the weekends too. At noon he stops work for half an hour to have his lunch. He eats cheese and tomato sandwiches which Betty makes for him, and drinks a bottle of milk. After lunch he works until four o'clock and then he goes home. In the evening he plays with his baby son, Ben, and watches TV with Betty. At ten thirty they all go to bed because they are very tired - and because they get up so early in the morning!"],"q":[["Tim wakes up at ...",["five o'clock","five thirty","six o'clock","a quarter after six"],1],["For breakfast, Betty has ...",["tea and cornflakes","coffee and toast","a bottle of milk","cheese and tomato sandwiches"],0],["Tim brushes his teeth before he gets dressed because ...",["he is always late","his wife asks him to","he doesn't want toothpaste on his uniform","the bathroom is busy later"],2],["Tim's job is ...",["bus driver","ticket seller","police officer","train driver"],3],["Tim's lunch break lasts ...",["fifteen minutes","half an hour","one hour","two hours"],1],["In the evening, Tim ...",["plays with his son and watches TV","goes back to work","reads the newspaper","cooks dinner for his family"],0]]},{"t":"Clarkson","n":198,"p":["Clarkson is a large town. It has more than fifty thousand people living there. It sits next to a large river, the Clark River. Every day, people take the ferry from North Clarkson to South Clarkson to go to work.","Most people live in North Clarkson. There are many trees and the streets are very broad. When the sun shines and the children run and play in the safe streets, North Clarkson is a wonderful place to live.","South Clarkson has a lot of shops and factories. People don't live there, but they come in the morning to work. There is also a big stadium where the Clarkson Tigers play. On Sundays, many people go to watch their favorite team.","At the weekend, the people from Clarkson enjoy walking along the bank of the Clark River. They also sail small boats on the water and if there is a little wind, they fly kites in the large park.","Every year, Clarkson grows in size because a lot of people come to live there. The government builds more houses and the streets get busier. Maybe in the future, if the town continues to grow, it will become a city!"],"q":[["More than ... people live in Clarkson.",["five thousand","fifteen thousand","fifty thousand","five hundred thousand"],2],["People go from North Clarkson to South Clarkson by ...",["bus","ferry","train","bicycle"],1],["Most people in Clarkson live in ...",["North Clarkson","South Clarkson","the stadium area","villages near the town"],0],["South Clarkson has a lot of ...",["trees and broad streets","houses and schools","parks and gardens","shops and factories"],3],["Many people watch the Clarkson Tigers on ...",["Fridays","Saturdays","Sundays","Mondays"],2],["If the town continues to grow, it may become a ...",["village","city","capital","country"],1]]},{"t":"A Zoo Keeper","n":329,"p":["My name is Susanna, and I work at the zoo. I am a zoo keeper. I love my job because I work with animals every day.","At the zoo, we have many animals. We have lions, tigers, elephants, and even penguins! My job is to take care of the animals and make sure they are happy and healthy.","Every morning, I start work at 7 o'clock. I arrive at the zoo, and the first thing I do is feed the animals. The lions and tigers eat meat, and the elephants eat fruit and vegetables. The penguins eat fish.","After I feed the animals, I clean their areas. The zoo must be clean for the animals and for the people who visit. Sometimes, I see visitors when I clean, and they ask me questions. They like to learn about the animals.","At 10 o'clock, the zoo opens to the public. Many families come to see the animals. I give talks about the animals during the day. I tell people what the animals eat, where they live in the wild, and what they like to do.","Right now, I am taking care of a baby elephant. She is very cute! She likes to play with her ball, and she drinks a lot of milk! I feed her every 3 hours.","My job is not easy, but it is fun. I also have to watch the animals carefully. If an animal is sick, I call the vet. The vet helps the animals when they are not well.","In the afternoon, I check all the animals again. I make sure they have enough food and water. Sometimes, I watch the lions play or see the penguins swim in their pool. I enjoy watching the animals because they are very active in the afternoon.","I finish work at 5 o'clock. When I go home, I am tired, but I am happy. I love working with animals, and I love my job at the zoo."],"q":[["Susanna works as a ...",["vet","tour guide","zoo keeper","teacher"],2],["Susanna starts work at ...",["5 o'clock","7 o'clock","9 o'clock","10 o'clock"],1],["The penguins at the zoo eat ...",["fish","meat","fruit","vegetables"],0],["The zoo opens to the public at ...",["7 o'clock","8 o'clock","9 o'clock","10 o'clock"],3],["Susanna feeds the baby elephant every ...",["hour","two hours","three hours","five hours"],2],["When an animal is sick, Susanna ...",["gives it medicine herself","calls the vet","closes the zoo","takes it home"],1]]},{"t":"My House","n":104,"p":["My house has a lot of rooms. It has a dining room where we eat food. My house also has a living room. My family watches TV in the living room. I sleep in my bedroom where my bed, clothes, and shoes are. My parents, brother, and sister each have their own bedroom.","I have two bathrooms in my house too. One bathroom is big and the other bathroom is small. Out of all the rooms in my house, we like the kitchen the most. It is where we make food and talk with each other. My house is my favorite place to be."],"q":[["The family eats food in the ...",["kitchen","living room","bedroom","dining room"],3],["The family watches TV in the ...",["living room","dining room","kitchen","bedroom"],0],["The house has ... bathrooms.",["one","two","three","four"],1],["The writer's parents, brother and sister ...",["share one bedroom","sleep in the living room","each have their own bedroom","live in another house"],2],["The room the family likes the most is the ...",["dining room","big bathroom","living room","kitchen"],3],["The writer's favorite place to be is ...",["the garden","his or her house","school","a friend's house"],1]]},{"t":"Studying in Brighton","n":213,"p":["I am studying English in Brighton this year. It is very different from my city in Spain! The weather here is cold and it often rains. In my city, it is always hot and sunny.","Brighton is next to the sea, and I love walking on the beach. The beach is not like my beach at home - it has stones, not sand! Many people swim in the sea but the water is very cold. I only put my feet in the water one time.","The city centre is very busy. There are lots of shops and restaurants. The food is different here. I like fish and chips but I miss Spanish food. My favourite cafe is near my school. They make very good tea and the colour inside is blue and white. It is small but pretty.","I live with an English family. They are very nice and help me with my English. Their house is not in the centre - it is 20 minutes by bus. The buses here are red and they come every 10 minutes. In my city in Spain, the buses are blue and we wait a long time!","I think Brighton is a good place to study. It is not too big and not too small. Perfect!"],"q":[["The writer comes from ...",["Italy","France","Spain","Portugal"],2],["The weather in Brighton is often ...",["cold and rainy","hot and sunny","warm and dry","snowy"],0],["The beach in Brighton has ...",["sand","stones","grass","shells"],1],["The writer's favourite cafe is near ...",["the beach","the bus stop","the family's house","the school"],3],["The English family's house is ... from the centre.",["10 minutes on foot","10 minutes by bus","20 minutes by bus","20 minutes by train"],2],["The buses in Brighton are ...",["red","blue","green","white"],0]]},{"t":"My Pets","n":125,"p":["I have three pets. One pet is a cat. My one cat likes to sleep a lot.","I have two more pets. Those two pets are dogs. The two dogs run a lot. They like to play a lot too. Both dogs are a lot busier than the one cat.","My oldest dog has brown fur. He is seven years old. My other dog is one. He has black and white fur. The two dogs sometimes play with another dog. Then there are three dogs. The back yard is busy with three dogs.","My cat likes to be alone. It does not want to play with other cats. I don't know why it is like that.","My one cat and two dogs make me happy."],"q":[["The writer has ... pets.",["two","three","four","five"],1],["The cat likes to ...",["run a lot","play with dogs","sleep a lot","play with other cats"],2],["The oldest dog is ... years old.",["one","three","five","seven"],3],["The younger dog has ... fur.",["black and white","brown","grey","white"],0],["When another dog comes to play, the back yard is ...",["quiet","empty","busy","dirty"],2],["The cat likes to be ...",["with the dogs","alone","with other cats","outside"],1]]},{"t":"Amy's Morning","n":209,"p":["Amy didn't want to go to school. Her best friend was sick and she was worried she wouldn't have anyone to sit by at lunch. She decided to pretend she was sick, so she stayed in bed when her dad called her to come eat breakfast.","When Amy didn't come down for breakfast, her dad went to her room to make sure she was awake. When he saw that she was awake but not answering, he started to get angry. Then he saw that Amy looked sad. Amy told her dad she had a stomachache, so he asked about her symptoms and felt her forehead to see if she had a fever. Her forehead didn't feel hot.","Amy's dad knew that her best friend wouldn't be in school, so he asked if this was the real reason she didn't want to go to school. Amy started to cry and said yes. Her dad hugged her and said he understood how hard it was but that maybe she could try to sit by someone new. He said maybe it wouldn't be as bad as she thought.","Finally, Amy decided to go to school. She found a new friend that day and was glad that she had listened to her dad."],"q":[["Amy didn't want to go to school because ...",["she had a test","her best friend was sick","she was really ill","she woke up late"],1],["Amy pretended that she was ...",["asleep","busy","sick","hungry"],2],["Amy told her dad that she had a ...",["stomachache","headache","fever","cold"],0],["Amy's dad felt her forehead, and it ...",["was very hot","was a little warm","was wet","didn't feel hot"],3],["Amy's dad said that she could try to ...",["stay at home","call her best friend","sit by someone new","eat lunch alone"],2],["At school that day, Amy ...",["felt sick","found a new friend","sat alone at lunch","went home early"],1]]}],[{"t":"Going to the Mall","n":233,"p":["Every city in the USA has a mall or four. There are about 1,100 malls in the US. These giant store-filled buildings are one of America's greatest entertainment options for teens and families.","Mall of America in Minnesota is the largest mall. It is so big that it has an amusement park in it, Nickelodeon Universe, with several roller coasters! There are more than 500 stores in it, about 400,000m2 and about 12,000 people work there.","But malls nowadays aren't the same as they used to be in the 80s and 90s. During those decades, teens would go to the mall and spend hours shopping, playing video games at the arcade, or just hanging out with friends. It was cool in the summer and warm in the winter. In those days, malls provided the perfect source of shopping and entertainment for entire families.","Nowadays, you can still eat in the food court and go to the movies, but there aren't many arcades left. Millennials have their Xboxes or Ps4s, and they like shopping online, which is also happening with more and more people from other generations. And as more people shop online and play video games at home, fewer people go to the mall to hang out.","Hopefully, malls will remain one of the great American pastimes. But only time will tell how people in the future will prefer to spend their time."],"q":[["There are about ... malls in the US.",["500","1,100","12,000","400,000"],1],["The largest mall in the USA is in ...",["Minnesota","New York","California","Nevada"],0],["Inside Mall of America there is ...",["a zoo","a stadium","an airport","an amusement park"],3],["In the 80s and 90s, teens at the mall often ...",["shopped online","watched Nickelodeon","played video games at the arcade","worked in the stores"],2],["Today fewer people hang out at malls because they ...",["have no money","shop online and play games at home","don't like food courts","live far from malls"],1],["The writer hopes that malls will ...",["become smaller","close soon","move online","remain a great American pastime"],3]]},{"t":"The History of Cartoons","n":290,"p":["Do you like watching cartoons? Probably you do! But how did they come to be? Who invented them?","This is actually a very tough question. The first cartoons were created long before the TV. For example, shadow play was a very popular form of entertainment in ancient China. Such shows looked almost like modern cartoons!","A toy called a flip book was made in the late 19th century. It was a small soft book with pictures. Each picture was drawn in a slightly different way. When you bend this book and release the pages one by one, the images start to move. Strictly speaking, they don't, but our eyes see it like that anyway. The first real cartoons were made using this trick, too!","In 1895 brothers Louis and Auguste Lumière created a cinematograph. It was a camera and a film projector in one device. Using this device, many aspiring film directors started to create their own cartoons.","This developed into a full industry by 1910. Many cartoons of that era are forgotten now, but some are still with us. For example, Felix the Cat was created by Otto Messmer in 1919, and he's still with us, more than a hundred years later. Currently the rights to the character are held by DreamWorks Animation.","One of the pioneers in the industry was famous Walt Disney. He was not afraid to experiment to make a cartoon, and his Snow White film was among the firsts to use a multiplane camera. With its help the characters were able to move around the objects, creating an illusion of a 3D world.","Today most of the cartoons are made with computer animation. The last traditional Disney cartoon to date was Winnie the Pooh (2011)."],"q":[["In ancient China, a popular form of entertainment was ...",["the flip book","shadow play","the cinematograph","computer animation"],1],["A flip book is ...",["a small book with slightly different pictures","a camera and a projector in one","a kind of shadow theatre","a magazine about cartoons"],0],["The cinematograph was created in ...",["1885","1910","1895","1919"],2],["Felix the Cat was created by ...",["Walt Disney","the Lumière brothers","DreamWorks Animation","Otto Messmer"],3],["The multiplane camera helped to create ...",["colour pictures","sound in cartoons","an illusion of a 3D world","computer animation"],2],["Today most cartoons are made with ...",["flip books","computer animation","multiplane cameras","shadow play"],1]]},{"t":"Homes for Rent","n":281,"p":["#A. House in the town centre","This lovely house is near all the shops and schools. There's one large bedroom, a small bedroom, and a bathroom on the first floor. On the ground floor, the kitchen has a fridge, oven, and dining table. There's a bright living room with a sofa and TV. It also comes with a garage and a small garden for you to enjoy.","#B. Big family home","On the ground floor, the small kitchen includes a fridge, dishwasher, and a washing machine. A large living room with a big sofa is perfect for relaxing with all the family. On the first floor, this house has 3 large bedrooms, each with a bed, wardrobe, desk and chair for homework. Outside, there's a big garden and a large garage.","#C. Small city apartment","This modern 1-bedroom apartment has a kitchen with a fridge, cooker, and washing machine. In the living room, there's an armchair, a coffee table, and a TV. The bedroom has a large bed and wardrobe. There's also a small balcony for fresh air. There's no parking space.","#D. Beautiful countryside house","This beautiful 4-bedroom house comes with a kitchen that includes an oven, fridge, and dishwasher, all downstairs. Upstairs, there's another bedroom. This area is quiet because it's far from the city. From the large windows you can see trees and mountains.","#E. Sunny large apartment","This bright 2-bedroom apartment has a large modern kitchen with a fridge, oven, and washing machine. There's a large dining room with a big table and ten chairs. The windows are large to let lots of sunlight in. There's no garden and no garage, but you have a parking space."],"q":[["The house in the town centre is near ...",["shops and schools","trees and mountains","the sea","a train station"],0],["In the big family home, each bedroom has ...",["a TV and a sofa","a balcony","a bed, wardrobe, desk and chair","its own bathroom"],2],["The small city apartment does NOT have ...",["a washing machine","a balcony","a TV","a parking space"],3],["The countryside house is quiet because ...",["it has thick walls","it is far from the city","nobody lives nearby","it has a big garden"],1],["From the windows of the countryside house you can see ...",["the town centre","a big garden","trees and mountains","a river"],2],["In the dining room of the sunny large apartment there are ...",["ten chairs","two sofas","four armchairs","six chairs"],0]]},{"t":"Time for an Adventure?","n":343,"p":["Are you a bit bored with your nine-to-five routine? Have a look at our exciting range of holidays and decide what type of adventure you'd like.","#Activity holidays","Our activity holidays are for everyone, people who love danger or who just like sports. We have a huge variety of water, snow or desert holidays. We'll take you SCUBA diving in the Red Sea or kayaking and white water rafting in Canada. If you prefer snow, you can try skiing or snowboarding in the Alps or even igloo-building. For those who like warmer weather, we also have sandboarding (the desert version of skateboarding) or camel safaris.","#Polar expeditions","Take a cruise to Antarctica or the northern Arctic; explore a land of white natural beauty and wonderful wildlife. Our experts will explain everything about the two poles as you watch the penguins in Antarctica or whales and polar bears in the Arctic. There's no greater adventure than travelling to the ends of the earth. A once-in-a-lifetime experience!","#Cultural journeys","Our cultural journeys will help you discover ancient civilisations: India, Thailand, Egypt and many more. Visit temples, palaces and ancient ruins – just remember to bring your camera! Get to know local ways of life by exploring markets, trying exotic foods and meeting local people.","#Trekking tours","We have trekking holidays to famous places such as Machu Picchu or the Everest Base Camp Trek, as well as some nearer to home in the Highlands of Scotland. You don't need to be very sporty, just fairly fit. You'll have a great time enjoying nature with a group of new friends. Some of the holidays include camping, but we'll transport the tents for you!","#Wildlife holidays","We organise small-group tours to get closer to nature in Africa, Asia or South America. Go on safari in Africa and watch lions and giraffes. Meet the famous turtles of the Galapagos Islands. Look for tigers in India, or take an elephant safari in Sri Lanka. We use local guides and stay in a range of accommodation, from tents to tree houses."],"q":[["The advertisement is for people who are bored with their ...",["holidays","hobbies","friends","nine-to-five routine"],3],["SCUBA diving is offered in ...",["Canada","the Red Sea","the Alps","the Galapagos Islands"],1],["Sandboarding is described as the desert version of ...",["skiing","surfing","skateboarding","kayaking"],2],["On a polar expedition to Antarctica you can watch ...",["penguins","polar bears","tigers","giraffes"],0],["For a trekking tour you need to be ...",["very sporty","fairly fit","an experienced climber","good at camping"],1],["On wildlife holidays, travellers may stay in ...",["palaces","igloos","cruise ships","tents or tree houses"],3]]},{"t":"First Jobs of Famous People","n":225,"p":["Not all famous people were born into famous families. A lot of them had normal lives before becoming famous. They also had jobs like the rest of us. Here are just a few:","Former President Obama worked at an ice-cream parlour selling ice-cream cones. He was in high school in Honolulu, Hawaii and said it was quite hard. He also said that the job taught him about responsibility, hard work and how to spend time with work, friends and family.","Brad Pitt had a weird job when he was younger. He worked as a chicken! That's right. He worked at a restaurant called El Pollo Loco in Hollywood, and his job was to dress up like a chicken and wave at the cars on the road. In fact, if you go on a “See the Stars” tour in Hollywood, many of them will take you past the same restaurant where Brad used to work.","Maybe the strangest path in life was that of Pope Francis. When he was young in Argentina, he worked several jobs before joining the Jesuits and dedicating his life to religion. He worked as a cleaner sweeping floors, a chemical tester at a laboratory and as a bouncer at a bar! Yes, the pope was the person responsible for security at a bar in case anything wrong happened. Can you imagine?!"],"q":[["As a teenager, Barack Obama worked ...",["in a restaurant kitchen","at a bar","at an ice-cream parlour","at a laboratory"],2],["Obama said that his first job taught him about ...",["responsibility and hard work","cooking","politics","saving money"],0],["Brad Pitt's job was to ...",["cook chicken","sell tickets for tours","drive tourists around Hollywood","dress up like a chicken and wave at cars"],3],["The restaurant where Brad Pitt worked is in ...",["Honolulu","Hollywood","Argentina","New York"],1],["Pope Francis grew up in ...",["Italy","Spain","Argentina","Hawaii"],2],["A bouncer is a person who is responsible for ...",["cleaning floors","security at a bar","testing chemicals","serving drinks"],1]]},{"t":"American Festivals","n":265,"p":["#Burning Man","Burning Man is an event that started in 1986 in Black Rock City, Nevada. People go to the desert and build a giant community for nine days. At the end of the festival, they burn a giant wooden statue of a man. That's where the name comes from. The festival celebrates inclusion, community, and responsibility. Everyone should share their talents so others can enjoy them for free. It is now also very popular with young influencers on social media and other famous people.","#The Taste of Buffalo","The Taste of Buffalo in Buffalo, New York, is the largest 2-day food festival in the country every year. Nearly half a million people visit the festival to eat food from over 50 restaurants. There are over 200 different dishes to try! Most of the food is from regional restaurants, and it is a great family event. You can listen to live music from 2 stages, walk through the parks and downtown as well as participate in a lot of different family-friendly activities.","#National Cherry Blossom Festival","The National Cherry Blossom Festival in Washington D.C. is a spring event that remembers the gift of cherry trees from Japan to the USA in 1912. The trees were a present from the mayor of Tokyo to the mayor of Washington DC. The first celebration was in 1934. There is a parade every year with large balloons, marching bands from all over the country, and concerts. There are also a lot of different shows and events, such as a kite flying festival, a 10-mile run, and fireworks at night."],"q":[["Burning Man started in ...",["1912","1934","1986","2012"],2],["The name \"Burning Man\" comes from ...",["the hot desert sun","the burning of a giant wooden statue","a famous song","the name of its founder"],1],["The Taste of Buffalo lasts ...",["two days","nine days","one week","one month"],0],["At the Taste of Buffalo you can try over ... different dishes.",["50","100","150","200"],3],["The cherry trees in Washington D.C. were a gift from ...",["China","Japan","Korea","France"],1],["The first National Cherry Blossom Festival was celebrated in ...",["1912","1986","1934","1943"],2]]},{"t":"My Neighborhood","n":263,"p":["My neighborhood is very peaceful and quiet. It is a new neighborhood, and there are a lot of big houses and some apartment buildings. The streets are very clean, and there aren't many cars. My school is near my house. I can walk there in 10 minutes.","There's also a park, which has a small playground for children and a large field. I like to play baseball there with my friends after school. All of the houses have a small front yard but big back yards. Most of my neighbors have a swimming pool, and some even have a trampoline!","Everyone has a flower garden, and in the spring, it is very beautiful. My road is called Maple Road. It is not in the city. It's in the suburbs. There aren't any restaurants, bars or cinemas on my road. But, if you go to Main street, you can find a lot of things to do. My favorite store is there. It's called Knick-Knack, and they sell everything you can imagine.","I like my neighborhood because it is very safe. There is no crime, and all of my neighbors take care of each other. The police station is next to the school, which is very nice. However, the hospital is quite far. It is in the city, and we have to drive 30 minutes to get there.","My friends live near me, and we often meet up to play or just talk. We love playing hockey in the street because there isn't any traffic. I think I am lucky to live in my neighborhood."],"q":[["The writer can walk to school in ...",["5 minutes","30 minutes","20 minutes","10 minutes"],3],["After school, the writer likes to ... in the park.",["play baseball","play hockey","ride a bike","swim"],0],["The writer's road is called ...",["Main Street","Park Road","Maple Road","Knick-Knack Road"],2],["The writer's favorite store is on ...",["Maple Road","Main Street","the road to the city","the school street"],1],["The police station is next to ...",["the park","the hospital","the store","the school"],3],["To get to the hospital, the family has to ...",["walk for 10 minutes","take a bus for an hour","drive for 30 minutes","go by train"],2]]},{"t":"Janet's Routine","n":270,"p":["Janet is an athlete, and she wakes up at 4:30 am every weekday morning. She spends the first 30 minutes reading and then 15 minutes meditating. At 5:15 am Janet checks her email for only 30 minutes and then goes for her first run of the day. She runs for an hour and a half along the lake near her house. After running, Janet has a shower and then prepares breakfast, which is usually cereal and fruit. However, she occasionally has a less healthy breakfast.","She usually finishes breakfast at around 8 am. If it is a weekday, she always leaves the house at 8:20 and goes to training. Her training starts at 9 am, and she needs 30 minutes to drive to the gym. She trains for 3 hours with her team and then goes home for lunch. She always eats a very big and healthy lunch. As soon as she finishes lunch, she has a nap for one hour.","After her nap, she likes to go for a walk around the lake and look at nature. She sometimes reads or meditates at the lake in the afternoon. In the evening, during the week, she meets up with friends. Most of her friends are athletes too, so they have a lot to talk about.","She typically goes to bed at 9 pm because she prefers to be awake in the morning than at night. She sometimes falls asleep listening to music, but she never watches the television or reads anything on her tablet. She always makes sure her alarm is set and is almost always asleep by 9:45 pm."],"q":[["Right after she wakes up, Janet spends 30 minutes ...",["reading","meditating","running","checking her email"],0],["Janet's first run of the day lasts ...",["30 minutes","45 minutes","one hour","an hour and a half"],3],["On weekdays, Janet leaves the house at ...",["8:00","8:20","8:30","9:00"],1],["Janet trains with her team for ...",["one hour","two hours","three hours","four hours"],2],["As soon as she finishes lunch, Janet ...",["has a nap","goes for a walk","meets friends","goes back to the gym"],0],["In bed, Janet never ...",["listens to music","sets her alarm","watches television","falls asleep before 10 pm"],2]]},{"t":"Bill Gates","n":122,"p":["Bill Gates was born in Seattle, Washington. He has accomplished a lot in the business world, but his rise to fame came when he developed Microsoft. He co-founded the company in 1975 with business partner and childhood friend, Paul Allen. Microsoft became the world's largest computer software company.","Bill Gates was chairman of the company and Chief Executive Officer (CEO). He stepped down from his CEO position in January 2000, but continued as chairman and chief software architect.","In February 2014 he stepped down as chairman of Microsoft and put more effort into a charity founded by him and his wife, The Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation. He also takes time to focus on climate change, global health and development, and education."],"q":[["Bill Gates was born in ...",["New York","Seattle","Los Angeles","Washington, D.C."],1],["Microsoft was founded in ...",["1957","1985","2000","1975"],3],["Paul Allen was Bill Gates's ...",["brother","teacher","childhood friend","first boss"],2],["In January 2000, Bill Gates stepped down as ...",["CEO","chairman","chief software architect","head of his charity"],0],["After February 2014, Bill Gates put more effort into ...",["a new software company","his charity foundation","teaching at a university","writing books"],1],["Bill Gates also takes time to focus on ...",["space travel","sport","computer games","climate change and global health"],3]]},{"t":"The Four Seasons","n":438,"p":["The four seasons are spring, summer, fall, and winter, and although various areas of the United States experience drastically different weather during these times, all portions of the country recognize the seasons; winter in California may bring heat, and winter in New York may bring blizzards, but both periods are nevertheless winter.","Following winter, spring begins on 20 March and ends on either 20 June or 21 June, in the United States (this date may vary slightly from year to year and hemisphere to hemisphere). For most, spring is a time of \"thawing,\" when the cold and snow of the winter are replaced by sunshine, reasonable temperatures, green grass, and more. It is also the season wherein previously dormant bees and butterflies reemerge, and when birds become more active.","Summer follows spring and spans from about 21 June to 22 September, in America. Summer is the warmest, the longest, and (arguably) the liveliest of the four seasons; students from kindergarten to college are given two or so summer months off from class, and to be sure, there are more young individuals out and about during this season than any other. It's not uncommon to see shorts, t-shirts, and sunglasses worn by those who're soaking up the summer's hot sun, and to stay cool, many individuals crank the air conditioning, take a dip in a swimming pool, and/or explore the ever-comforting ocean waves.","Autumn (or fall), more than being a simple precursor to winter, is one of the most beautiful and exciting seasons. Spanning from 22 September to 21 December (technically, that is; snow will begin to fall long before this latter date, in most parts of the country, leading many to classify the period as winter) in the US, autumn is characterized by falling leaves, Halloween (on 31 October), and Thanksgiving. During fall, traditional farmers harvest their produce, and the aforementioned falling leaves change to gorgeous orange, red, and yellow colors; these colors are generally associated with autumn itself, in turn.","In the US, winter spans from 21 December to 20 March, and is, as one would expect, the coldest of the four seasons. In the eastern, northern, and central portions of the country, winter snowfall can be considerable; in the western and southern portions of the country, winter snowfall is highly unlikely, but temperatures nevertheless drop from their usual. Winter is most widely known for its snow and the Christmas holiday, but it also boasts New Year's Eve (on December 31, in recognition of the start of a new year) and other special days. Ice skating, sledding, ice hockey, and snowball fights are commonly enjoyed winter activities."],"q":[["In the United States, spring begins on ...",["20 March","21 June","22 September","21 December"],0],["In spring, bees and butterflies ...",["fly south","disappear","reemerge","fall asleep"],2],["Summer is described as the ... of the four seasons.",["shortest","warmest and longest","most beautiful","quietest"],1],["The colors associated with autumn are ...",["green and blue","white and grey","pink and purple","orange, red and yellow"],3],["In winter, heavy snowfall is highly unlikely in the ... parts of the country.",["eastern","northern","western and southern","central"],2],["An activity commonly enjoyed in winter is ...",["ice skating","swimming in the ocean","harvesting produce","trick-or-treating"],0]]}],[{"t":"Inventions of the 1920s","n":400,"p":["The 1920s was an exciting time for inventions. Some of the things invented around that time changed the lives of millions of people, and some of those inventions are still widely used today.","#The television","The invention with the biggest impact was probably the television. It was invented by a Scottish man, John Logie Baird. The first televised pictures were sent over a short distance in 1924, and his invention was formally demonstrated at the Royal Institute two years later. The pictures on the screen were not clear, but the viewers could see that they were human faces, and they could see their eyes opening and closing. In 1928, images were sent from Britain to America, and later, to a ship 1,500 miles out to sea. In the same year, the first colour images were sent. The first ‘seeing-in sets' were sold that same year. For £25 (£1000 in today's money), people in their homes could watch moving images that were sent from a broadcasting station.","#The fridge","Another invention that became popular in the 1920s was the home refrigerator. People used different ways to keep food cool and fresh long before the 1900s, but home fridges weren't invented until 1913 in the USA. Home fridges became very popular in the USA in the 1920s. Sales of the popular ‘Frigidaire' model increased from 5,000 in 1921 to 750,000 in 1926. British people were less interested in fridges than Americans. They thought that they were unnecessary because the weather in Britain was cooler. But fridges were heavily advertised, and their advantages were described in detail. Soon, more fridges were sold, and the price decreased.","#The polygraph","Another interesting invention of the 1920s was the polygraph, or lie detector. It was invented in 1921 by a Californian policeman, John Larson. He used the ideas of other psychologists to make a machine that measured people's heart rate, breathing and blood pressure while they were asked questions. The experts believed that sudden changes in these measurements showed that someone was lying. Although this invention is well-known, it can't really detect lies. Marston tried to use measurements from his polygraph in a court case in 1923, but they weren't accepted as evidence and never have been since then. However, polygraphs are still used by some police forces and the FBI because many people believe they work, so they tell the truth to avoid the machine."],"q":[["The television was invented by ...",["John Larson","an American engineer","a Californian policeman","John Logie Baird"],3],["The first colour images were sent in ...",["1924","1926","1928","1921"],2],["In 1928, a \"seeing-in set\" cost ...",["£25","£100","£750","£1,000"],0],["At first, British people were less interested in fridges because ...",["fridges were too expensive","the weather in Britain was cooler","fridges were not advertised","they did not trust American products"],1],["The polygraph measures a person's ...",["voice and eye movements","body temperature","heart rate, breathing and blood pressure","brain activity"],2],["According to the text, polygraphs are still used because ...",["courts accept their results","they are cheap","they always detect lies","many people believe they work and tell the truth"],3]]},{"t":"Why Do People Collect?","n":342,"p":["Petra Engels owns 19,571 erasers, Carol Vaughn has 1,221 bars of soap, and Ralf Shrőder has a collection of 14,502 packets of sugar. Many people love to collect things, but why? Psychologists and collectors have different opinions.","The psychologist Carl Jung believed that collecting is part of our ancient human history. Thousands of years ago, humans collected nuts and berries. They kept them carefully and ate them when there was no food. The best collectors survived long cold winters or seasons without rain. Their genes passed to future generations. Nowadays, we still have a collecting instinct.","Historian Philipp Bloom has a different opinion. He thinks collectors want to make something that will remain after their death. By bringing many similar items together, the collector gains historical importance. Sometimes their collections become museums or libraries, for example, Henry Huntington, who founded a library in Los Angeles to house his collection of books.","Author Steve Roach thinks that people collect things to remember their childhood. Many children collect things, but few have enough money to buy the things they really want, and they lose interest. In later life, they remember their collections fondly. Now, they have enough money and opportunity to find special items, and they start collecting again. This way, they can re-live and enjoy their childhood years.","Art collector, Werner Muensterberger, agrees that collecting is linked to childhood. But he believes we collect in order to feel safe and secure. While babies hold blankets or toys to feel safe when their mother isn't there, adults collect things to stop feeling lonely or anxious.","Autograph collector Mark Baker agrees that collecting is emotional, but he doesn't collect to reduce anxiety. “For me, it's the excitement,” he says. “I love trying to get a famous person's autograph. Sometimes I succeed, and sometimes I fail. Also, by collecting autographs, I feel connected to famous people. I don't just watch them on television. I actually meet them.”","These are just a few reasons for collecting. Do you know any people with collections? Why do they collect?"],"q":[["Ralf Shrőder collects ...",["erasers","packets of sugar","bars of soap","autographs"],1],["According to Carl Jung, people collect things because ...",["it is part of our ancient human history","they want to become famous","they miss their childhood","they feel lonely"],0],["Philipp Bloom thinks that collectors want to ...",["earn a lot of money","meet famous people","leave something that will remain after their death","feel safe"],2],["Henry Huntington founded a ... to house his collection.",["museum","school","gallery","library"],3],["According to Steve Roach, adults start collecting again because they ...",["have nothing else to do","now have enough money and opportunity","want to sell the items later","need to reduce anxiety"],1],["For Mark Baker, the main reason for collecting autographs is ...",["safety","history","excitement","money"],2]]},{"t":"Hand Luggage Rules","n":144,"p":["Please note that passengers can only take ONE suitcase onto the plane. It must be no bigger than 55cm x 22cm x 35cm and weigh no more than 10kg.","You can also take one small laptop bag or handbag that can fit under the seat in front of you. If you have two bags, their total weight cannot be more than 10kg. If your bag is too big or too heavy, you will not be allowed to take it onto the plane. Staff will put it in the hold for you and you will have to pay extra.","Please make sure mobile phones and other devices are fully charged so that security staff can check them.","Liquids in bottles bigger than 100ml are allowed on board if you buy them in the airport shops after you've passed security.","We hope you enjoy your flight!"],"q":[["Each passenger can take ... onto the plane.",["one suitcase","two suitcases","three bags","no suitcases"],0],["The suitcase must weigh no more than ...",["5 kg","15 kg","20 kg","10 kg"],3],["A small laptop bag or handbag must fit ...",["in the overhead locker","under the seat in front of you","inside the suitcase","in the hold"],1],["If your bag is too big or too heavy, ...",["you can leave it at the gate for free","you cannot fly","staff will put it in the hold and you will pay extra","you can carry it on your knees"],2],["Mobile phones should be fully charged so that ...",["you can use them during the flight","you can call the airline","you can show your ticket","security staff can check them"],3],["Bottles bigger than 100 ml are allowed on board if ...",["you bought them in the airport shops after security","they are in your suitcase","they contain only water","you show them to the staff"],0]]},{"t":"The Record-Breaker","n":353,"p":["Ashrita Furman is a record-breaking record-breaker: He has set more records than anyone else in the world! In the last 40 years, he has established more than 600 records!","As a child, Ashrita loved reading the Guinness Book of World Records. He wanted to be in it too. But he thought people had to be good at sports to be in the book. He wasn't a good athlete. He felt his dream would never come true.","But later in life, Ashrita learned meditation, and with this, he learned that nothing is impossible. He tested this idea in 1978 by entering a 24-hour bike race in New York. Without any training, Ashrita came third! After that, he started thinking about breaking records again.","First, he tried to break the record for the most jumping jacks. He failed at first, but, remembering that ‘anything is possible', he trained, meditated and tried again. This time, he did 27,000 jumping jacks in 6 hours 45 minutes and became the new record holder. His achievement appeared in the 1980 Guinness Book of Records.","Today, Ashrita has a long list of records, including: walking the furthest distance with a bike on his chin, cycling the longest distance with a bottle on his head and lighting the most candles on a birthday cake. He says: “I choose ideas which are challenging, fun and childish! I enjoy practising and seeing my progress.”","He says his favourite record was “the longest distance on a pogo stick”. While he was on holiday in Japan, he saw Mount Fuji and thought it was beautiful, so he decided to try to break a record there. He did 11.5 miles. The most difficult record was “the most forward rolls”. In 10 hours, 30 minutes, he did 8,341 of them, travelling 12 miles!","If you want to break a world record too, Ashrita gives this advice. “Choose something you enjoy because you will need to practise. And don't give up. Your mind will tell you that something is impossible, but it isn't. If someone else has done something, and you work hard, you can do it too!"],"q":[["In the last 40 years, Ashrita Furman has set more than ... records.",["400","600","1,980","8,000"],1],["As a child, Ashrita thought he could never be in the Guinness Book because he ...",["was too young","was too shy","wasn't a good athlete","didn't have enough money"],2],["In the 24-hour bike race in 1978, Ashrita ...",["came third","won","did not finish","broke a world record"],0],["His first record was for ...",["forward rolls","pogo stick jumping","cycling with a bottle on his head","jumping jacks"],3],["His favourite record was set ...",["in New York","near Mount Fuji in Japan","in London","in his home town"],1],["Ashrita's advice to future record-breakers is to ...",["train with a professional coach","choose the easiest record","choose something you enjoy and not give up","start when you are a child"],2]]},{"t":"Romeo the Frog","n":324,"p":["Romeo the frog spent ten years alone in a museum in Bolivia. He is a Sehuencas frog, and for a long time, scientists at the museum thought that Romeo was the only Sehuencas frog in the world. A lot of these frogs have died because of deforestation,and a deadly disease has taken the lives of many others. The scientists wanted to find a mate for Romeo. They even set up a profile for him on Match.com!","In 2018, some scientists went to the Bolivian forests to look for a partner for Romeo. They looked in many streams, but they couldn't find any frogs. They grew tired, wet and disappointed. They decided to check one more stream before going home. And then, they found a Sehuencas frog beside a waterfall. This frog was male, but they returned the next day and found four more frogs, two male and two female. One of the females was the right age to mate. The conservationists called her Juliet.","The scientists didn't let Romeo and Juliet meet at first. They needed to check that Juliet didn't have a disease. But finally, they put the two frogs together. At first, Romeo seemed nervous, but soon he swam to Juliet and then he did a funny dance, shaking his toes. Then he put his arms and legs around her and made a really loud call.","Juliet hasn't laid any eggs yet, but scientists hope she will. That way, they can learn more about these frogs and help to protect them in the forest. But they know it won't be easy. For frogs to mate, the temperature and the water quality must be perfect. They also only mate when it is raining, so the scientists had to put special rain equipment in their tank. However, the scientists aren't too worried if Romeo and Juliet don't mate, because they have the four other frogs. And they are happy that Romeo isn't lonely anymore!"],"q":[["Romeo lived alone in a museum for ...",["two years","five years","eight years","ten years"],3],["Many Sehuencas frogs have died because of ...",["deforestation and a deadly disease","hunting","cold winters","pollution from museums"],0],["The scientists found the first frog ...",["in a museum tank","in a lake","beside a waterfall","under a tree"],2],["Altogether, the scientists found ... frogs in the forest.",["two","five","four","ten"],1],["Romeo and Juliet did not meet at first because ...",["Romeo was too nervous","Juliet was too young","the tank was too small","scientists had to check that Juliet didn't have a disease"],3],["Sehuencas frogs only mate when ...",["it is raining","it is very hot","it is dark","they are alone"],0]]},{"t":"Generations Online","n":431,"p":["Today's grandparents are joining their grandchildren on social media, but the different generations' online habits couldn't be more different. In the UK the over-55s are joining Facebook in increasing numbers, meaning that they will soon be the site's second biggest user group, with 3.5 million users aged 55–64 and 2.9 million over-65s.","Sheila, aged 59, says, 'I joined to see what my grandchildren are doing, as my daughter posts videos and photos of them. It's a much better way to see what they're doing than waiting for letters and photos in the post. That's how we did it when I was a child, but I think I'm lucky I get to see so much more of their lives than my grandparents did.'","Ironically, Sheila's grandchildren are less likely to use Facebook themselves. Children under 17 in the UK are leaving the site – only 2.2 million users are under 17 – but they're not going far from their smartphones. Chloe, aged 15, even sleeps with her phone. 'It's my alarm clock so I have to,' she says. 'I look at it before I go to sleep and as soon as I wake up.'","Unlike her grandmother's generation, Chloe's age group is spending so much time on their phones at home that they are missing out on spending time with their friends in real life. Sheila, on the other hand, has made contact with old friends from school she hasn't heard from in forty years. 'We use Facebook to arrange to meet all over the country,' she says. 'It's changed my social life completely.'","Teenagers might have their parents to thank for their smartphone and social media addiction as their parents were the early adopters of the smartphone. Peter, 38 and father of two teenagers, reports that he used to be on his phone or laptop constantly. 'I was always connected and I felt like I was always working,' he says. 'How could I tell my kids to get off their phones if I was always in front of a screen myself?' So, in the evenings and at weekends, he takes his SIM card out of his smartphone and puts it into an old-style mobile phone that can only make calls and send text messages. 'I'm not completely cut off from the world in case of emergencies, but the important thing is I'm setting a better example to my kids and spending more quality time with them.'","Is it only a matter of time until the generation above and below Peter catches up with the new trend for a less digital life?"],"q":[["Sheila joined Facebook to ...",["find a new job","see what her grandchildren are doing","play online games","read the news"],1],["When Sheila was a child, families shared news and photos ...",["by phone","by email","by post","on television"],2],["Chloe sleeps with her phone because ...",["it is her alarm clock","she is afraid of the dark","her parents ask her to","she plays games at night"],0],["Thanks to Facebook, Sheila has ...",["started a business","moved to another city","learned to use a smartphone","made contact with old school friends"],3],["In the evenings and at weekends, Peter ...",["turns his phone off completely","uses an old-style mobile phone","gives his phone to his children","works on his laptop"],1],["Peter changed his habits because he wanted to ...",["save money","work more","set a better example to his kids","sleep better"],2]]},{"t":"Mike's Story","n":355,"p":["My name is Mike, and I was born in a very poor neighborhood in Los Angeles. When I was little, my mother took care of my brother and me because my father left before I came to this world, and he wasn't too interested in being a part of our lives. My mother had to work two jobs that didn't pay much. She had to work very hard so that we could have food, clothes, and a place to live. She was great; we loved her and appreciated her efforts.","Every day, she usually arrived home very late, and she was exhausted. Still, she always cooked nice meals for us, played with us, and made sure that we did our homework. She always insisted on how important it was to get a good education. She couldn't finish school because she got pregnant with me when she was just a teenager. “I couldn't finish school, and that's why I must work so hard now, and I earn so little money,” she often said.","Children were very violent in my neighborhood; you needed to be violent if you wanted to survive. There was a lot of crime, vandalism, and drugs. It wasn't easy to be a good student for any child who lived in our neighborhood. The other children hated you if you got good grades, so I wasn't very popular. The only positive thing about living in that place is that you needed to be very clever to survive, and that helped me later in life.","Finally, my brother and I could finish high school. I went to university because I was a very good basketball player, and basketball paid for my university degree. On the day of my graduation, my mother was in tears. And she was in tears again the day I showed her our new apartment, away from the neighborhood. Do you know how difficult it is to get out of a poor neighborhood? It's very hard, but I did it, and it was thanks to my mum. Now she doesn't have to work two jobs anymore, and I take care of her."],"q":[["Mike was born in a poor neighborhood in ...",["New York","Chicago","Los Angeles","San Francisco"],2],["Mike's father ...",["left before Mike was born","worked two jobs","died when Mike was little","lived in another country"],0],["Mike's mother couldn't finish school because she ...",["had to work","was not a good student","moved to another city","got pregnant as a teenager"],3],["In Mike's neighborhood, other children hated you if you ...",["were poor","got good grades","played basketball","were violent"],1],["Mike was able to go to university because he was ...",["from a rich family","the best student in his class","a very good basketball player","helped by his father"],2],["Mike's mother was in tears when ...",["he graduated and when he showed her the new apartment","he left the neighborhood alone","he lost a basketball game","she lost her job"],0]]},{"t":"Chinese New Year","n":230,"p":["Chinese New Year is a major holiday in Greater China and has strongly influenced lunar new year celebrations of China's neighbouring cultures, including the Korean New Year (seol), the Tết of Vietnam, and the Losar of Tibet. It is also celebrated worldwide in regions and countries with significant Overseas Chinese populations, including Singapore, Indonesia, Malaysia, Myanmar, Thailand, Cambodia, the Philippines, and Mauritius, as well as many in North America and Europe.","Chinese New Year is associated with several myths and customs. The festival was traditionally a time to honour divinities as well as ancestors. Within China, regional customs and traditions concerning the celebration of the New Year vary widely, and the evening preceding Chinese New Year's Day is frequently regarded as an occasion for Chinese families to gather for the annual reunion dinner. It is also traditional for every family to thoroughly clean their house, in order to sweep away any ill-fortune and to make way for incoming good luck. Another custom is the decoration of windows and doors with red paper-cuts and two-line poems. Popular themes among these paper-cuts and two-line poems include that of good fortune or happiness, wealth, and longevity. Other activities include lighting firecrackers and giving money in red paper envelopes. For the northern regions of China, dumplings are featured prominently in meals celebrating the festival. It often serves as the first meal of the year."],"q":[["The Korean New Year is called ...",["Tết","Losar","Seol","Mauritius"],2],["Traditionally, the festival was a time to honour ...",["emperors","divinities and ancestors","teachers","children"],1],["On the evening before New Year's Day, Chinese families usually ...",["go to the theatre","travel abroad","visit their neighbours","gather for the annual reunion dinner"],3],["Families thoroughly clean their houses in order to ...",["sweep away ill-fortune","prepare for guests from abroad","save money","decorate the windows"],0],["Windows and doors are decorated with ...",["green leaves","white flowers","red paper-cuts and two-line poems","gold coins"],2],["In the northern regions of China, people traditionally eat ... during the festival.",["noodles","dumplings","rice cakes","fish"],1]]},{"t":"Black Friday","n":338,"p":["The day after Thanksgiving has become America's biggest shopping day. Closed all day on Thursday, shopping centres all across the nation open early on Friday. Some of them open at 12:01 Friday morning, while others open at 4 a.m. Some “sleepyhead“shopping centres, like Target this year, don't open their doors on Friday until 6 a.m. From Friday to the day before Christmas, this is the season when businesses make nearly 25 per cent of what they earn in a year. This season puts many businesses “in the black”, that is, they make the money they need for the year.","Reporters from local TV stations interview people who sleep in tents in front of the shops a day or two before the doors open on Friday. These people patiently wait in queue to get products that are 50 per cent cheaper or more.","“Oh, we have fun,” said one of the persons queuing. “We sometimes bring games to play, we watch TV and order lots of pizza, and we often meet interesting people. And, most important of all, we save a lot!” The problem, of course, is that only a very small number of products have big reductions in their prices. Apart from a few big discounts, each shop has other things that are reduced from 10 to 50 per cent, saving shoppers from $10 to $400 per item, and so Americans want to go shopping.","Not all Americans enjoy shopping. Reverend William Graham wants to change Black Friday's name. “We want to call it Remember Jesus Friday. People should start the season with the right attitude. Christmas has become a Season of Shopping. We want to make it a Season of Giving. And we don't mean giving material things. We mean giving your back, your mind, and your hands. Help an old lady clean up her house. Teach a kid how to read. Visit sick people in the hospital or in nursing homes. Give food to the Red Cross. Celebrate Christmas by remembering Jesus and forgetting Santa Claus.”"],"q":[["America's biggest shopping day is the day after ...",["Christmas","Halloween","New Year","Thanksgiving"],3],["From Black Friday to Christmas, businesses make nearly ... of their yearly income.",["10 per cent","25 per cent","50 per cent","75 per cent"],1],["\"In the black\" means that businesses ...",["make the money they need for the year","lose money","close their doors","open at night"],0],["Some people sleep in tents in front of the shops in order to ...",["meet TV reporters","protest against shopping","get products that are 50 per cent cheaper or more","sell pizza"],2],["The problem with Black Friday is that ...",["shops are closed in the morning","only a very small number of products have big reductions","the queues are too short","prices are higher than usual"],1],["Reverend William Graham wants Christmas to be a season of ...",["shopping","resting","travelling","giving"],3]]},{"t":"How to Spot Fake News","n":413,"p":["Every time you're online, you are bombarded by pictures, articles, links and videos trying to tell their story. Unfortunately, not all of these stories are true. Sometimes they want you to click on another story or advertisement at their own site, other times they want to upset people for political reasons. These days it's so easy to share information. These stories circulate quickly, and the result is … fake news.","There is a range of fake news: from crazy stories which people easily recognise to more subtle types of misinformation. Experts in media studies and online psychology have been examining the fake news phenomenon. Read these tips, and don't get fooled!","#1. Check the source","Look at the website where the story comes from. Does it look real? Is the text well written? Are there a variety of other stories or is it just one story? Fake news websites often use addresses that sound like real newspapers, but don't have many real stories about other topics. If you aren't sure, click on the 'About' page and look for a clear description of the organisation.","#2. Watch out for fake photos","Many fake news stories use images that are Photoshopped or taken from an unrelated site. Sometimes, if you just look closely at an image, you can see if it has been changed. Or use a tool like Google Reverse Image search. It will show you if the same image has been used in other contexts.","#3. Check the story is in other places","Look to see if the story you are reading is on other news sites that you know and trust. If you do find it on many other sites, then it probably isn't fake (although there are some exceptions), as many big news organisations try to check their sources before they publish a story.","#4. Look for other signs","There are other techniques that fake news uses. These include using ALL CAPS and lots of ads that pop up when you click on a link. Also, think about how the story makes you feel. If the news story makes you angry, it's probably designed to make you angry.","If you know these things about online news, and can apply them in your everyday life, then you have the control over what to read, what to believe and most importantly what to share. If you find a news story that you know is fake, the most important advice is: don't share it!"],"q":[["According to the text, fake news spreads quickly because ...",["newspapers print it","people don't read online","it is so easy to share information","experts support it"],2],["Fake news websites often use addresses that ...",["sound like real newspapers","are very long","end in .com","contain numbers"],0],["If you aren't sure about a website, you should ...",["share the story","click on the ads","write to the author","look at the \"About\" page"],3],["Google Reverse Image search shows you ...",["who took the photo","if the same image has been used in other contexts","how to edit a photo","how old the photo is"],1],["If a story appears on many news sites that you trust, it ...",["is certainly fake","is always true","probably isn't fake","is an advertisement"],2],["The most important advice about a fake news story is ...",["don't share it","report it to the police","comment on it","read it twice"],0]]}],[{"t":"Four Cities","n":554,"p":["#A. Trinka","While studying here, I stayed in an apartment in Saint Catherine's Square. In medieval times, this would have been a bustling area used for markets and public gatherings, but now it's a little haven of calm, away from traffic and commerce. There are places like this dotted around the city, most of which have benches where you can rest your feet. There's no greenery in them, though. It would have been nice to get some shade from a tree or enjoy its blossom. The city as a whole lacks green spaces or paths beside the river where you can enjoy nature. Even so, under the canopy of a sidewalk café, with your back against a café window, you can detach yourself from the fast pace of city life and while away the hours even on the city's busiest streets.","#B. Kampela","As a city, Kampela is entirely utilitarian. The purpose of its streets is simply to shift people and goods from one place to another. Consequently, they are devoid of any aesthetic attributes, like statues or old decorative features. Since there's nothing of appeal to make you want to linger, everyone just puts their head down and gets where they want to be as quickly as possible. As a result, it doesn't give the impression of a relaxing city. That said, once you get where you want to be, you will often find yourself somewhere peaceful and serene. For there is no shortage of quiet tea shops and bars designed with soft lighting and comfortable furniture to calm your nerves. Many of these have outdoor spaces at the back, which are quiet and well-tended despite being overlooked by other buildings.","#C. Pokamarka","Urban planners in Pokamarka seem to have not considered that people might enjoy eating or drinking outside since wide pavements or pedestrianized zones which might house a bench or a café don't exist here. If you want to keep moving, though, you can get some quiet time in plenty of places. The routes along the canal are perfect for stretching the legs. Moreover, the city's park is barely used, though it's not terribly well maintained. It's hard to find somewhere to sit which looks both clean and able to support someone's weight. There's rather a lot of graffiti and vandalism, which has probably discouraged the local council from planting trees and flowers. The few that exist look rather unkempt.","#D. Melwick","From an aerial photograph, you would think that Melwick would have plenty of peaceful outdoor spaces. After all, its harbour location offers lots of paved walkways and open plazas, and Great Park is well-known for its stunning trees and botanical gardens, which are just as spectacular in real life as in pictures. The problem is that these spaces are always heaving with tourists trying to take selfies against the statues and memorials, so trying to relax there will make you stressed and irritated. Your best option is to get on a ferry or cable car at a quiet time of day. Then, you can watch the city drift past in peace. However, time it wrong and you'll be queuing next to a load of noisy school kids. Even the cafes don't give you any kind of refuge, as the servers want you in and out as quickly as possible."],"q":[["In medieval times, Saint Catherine's Square in Trinka was used for ...",["markets and public gatherings","military parades","growing food","religious ceremonies only"],0],["According to the text, Trinka as a whole lacks ...",["benches","sidewalk cafés","quiet squares","green spaces"],3],["The streets of Kampela are described as ...",["beautiful and historic","purely functional","full of statues","pleasant for walking"],1],["In Kampela, peace and quiet can be found in ...",["the streets","large parks","tea shops and bars","the harbour"],2],["In Pokamarka, the best place for stretching the legs is ...",["the pedestrianized zones","the routes along the canal","the city squares","the pavement cafés"],1],["The peaceful-looking outdoor spaces of Melwick are in fact ...",["closed to visitors","badly maintained","very expensive","crowded with tourists"],3]]},{"t":"Personality and Health","n":571,"p":["There is increasing evidence that health is linked to personality. However, until now, the relationship has not affected the way health care is delivered. There are several reasons for this. Some health workers doubt whether there is a direct link between health and personality or whether it's just a coincidence. Some feel it is their professional duty to treat all patients in the same way. Others argue that delivering health services according to patients' personalities will have minimal impact and therefore isn't worth the effort. However, some psychologists believe that applying different procedures to people with different personalities could have a significant, positive effect on health.","Research into personality has, in recent years, focused on the Big Five model of personality types. This model measures how neurotic, extrovert, open to experience, agreeable and conscientious a person is. Some of these personality types have been studied in relation to health. For example, conscientious people tend to be less likely to smoke, drink too much alcohol or be inactive. However, in other cases, the relationship is less clear. Neurotic behaviour, for instance, has been found in some studies to increase the risk of death, in others to protect people from illness and in others to have no link to health at all.","Even so, if health workers applied an understanding of personality to the services they provide, they could influence the extent to which patients act on advice and follow their treatment. For example, high sensation-seeking individuals, who are extroverts and unconscientious in the Big Five model and tend to take part in risky activities, respond to drama, energy and emotion. Thus, to encourage those people to follow health advice, health promotions can be designed to incorporate those factors. An example of this was the campaign SENTAR which aimed to reduce cannabis use among high sensation-seeking teenagers. By creating a suitable television advert, they successfully engaged these youths and reduced their recreational drug use. Of course, this approach isn't always possible. It is often impractical and expensive to create several versions of a campaign to reach different personality types. However, recent developments in computer technology, cookies and targeted advertising may allow this approach to be used more in future.","Personality could also be considered when sending messages, information and guidance to specific patients. Already, health information is usually available in various forms – printed, digital, audio, and so on – to be suitable and accessible for different users, such as the blind, the elderly, and people with reading difficulties. Research has also shown that, by identifying different patients' motivations for treatment and then corresponding with them in a way that reflects their motivations, patients will become more involved in their treatment, compared to when the same messages are sent to everyone. Correspondence could, therefore, be adapted to reflect patients' personality type, too. For example, less conscientious people could be sent phone reminders to attend appointments. So far, there has been very little research into the effectiveness of tailoring health guidance according to personality, so this area deserves further study.","Until now, the focus of personality-health research has been to explore the link between personality and health and has had very little practical application. Thus, health workers have not engaged deeply with it. However, by suggesting, trialling and implementing practices to engage patients with different personalities, the relationship between psychology researchers and health workers could improve, along with the health of the general public."],"q":[["So far, the link between health and personality has ...",["completely changed health care","been rejected by all psychologists","not affected the way health care is delivered","been proved to be a coincidence"],2],["The Big Five model measures how ... a person is.",["neurotic, extrovert, open, agreeable and conscientious","healthy, active, calm, social and careful","intelligent, creative, honest, brave and kind","rich, educated, sporty, friendly and young"],0],["Conscientious people tend to be ...",["more likely to take risks","more often ill","less interested in treatment","less likely to smoke or be inactive"],3],["The SENTAR campaign aimed to ...",["make teenagers do more sport","reduce cannabis use among sensation-seeking teenagers","teach doctors about personality","advertise new medicines"],1],["Creating several versions of a health campaign is often ...",["illegal","unnecessary","impractical and expensive","very effective and cheap"],2],["According to the text, less conscientious people could be sent ...",["phone reminders to attend appointments","printed brochures","longer letters","invitations to group therapy"],0]]},{"t":"Pets, Farms and Zoos","n":323,"p":["Many people like to keep animals as pets. It may come as a surprise, but studies based on animal populations in homes have revealed that fish are the most popular pet. This makes sense, however, because many people keep aquariums with multiple fish instead of just one. Second to fish, cats are the next most popular house pet, shortly followed by dogs. While these are the top three pets on the list, many people do keep other animals in their homes. Birds, reptiles, and other small animals are popular pets as well. Many children receive small pets to learn responsibility. Popular pets for children include hamsters, rabbits, guinea pigs, and even mice or rats.","Animals used for agriculture can vary from farm to farm. However, there are many common animals found on farms. Many farms typically raise chickens, cows, and even pigs. These animals are popular on farms because they are commonly used for food products like meat and dairy. However, there are other farms that have more diverse populations of animals. Some farms also raise sheep, goats, ducks, and horses. Traditionally, horses were common on farms because they helped with a lot of field work. Now, it is still common to see horses driving plows on some farms. However, in many cases, horses are now used for more recreational activities like riding or even for competing.","Zoos are another common space to find more exotic collections of animals. It is fun to spend a day at the zoo to observe different species. At the zoo, it is common to find animals living in exhibits that resemble their natural habitats. For instance, monkeys live in cages with plenty of vines and branches to climb. Lions tend to live in deep, pitted cages resembling savannahs. Elephants live separately in sprawling grasslands with plenty of trees. At the zoo, it's great to learn about animals from all over the world and their diverse natural environments."],"q":[["According to studies, the most popular pets are ...",["cats","dogs","fish","birds"],2],["Many children receive small pets in order to ...",["learn responsibility","earn money","study biology","have fun at school"],0],["Chickens, cows and pigs are popular on farms because they ...",["are easy to keep","are friendly","help with field work","are used for meat and dairy products"],3],["Traditionally, horses were common on farms because they ...",["were kept for riding competitions","helped with field work","were cheap to buy","protected other animals"],1],["At the zoo, animals usually live in exhibits that ...",["are as small as possible","are open to visitors","resemble their natural habitats","are shared with other species"],2],["In the zoo described in the text, elephants live ...",["in cages with vines and branches","in deep, pitted cages","next to the lions","in sprawling grasslands with trees"],3]]},{"t":"New York City","n":326,"p":["New York City is a thriving city in the state of New York, and is home to over 8,500,000 individuals; this means that \"The Big Apple\" (one of New York City's nicknames) boasts a population that's over twice as large as that of Los Angeles, California!","It isn't difficult to see why, as NYC has a long and storied history and is a bastion of culture (or societies' defining characteristics and practices), entertainment, and activities. To be sure, New York is widely considered to be a \"global\" city, as so many persons from so many corners of the globe live in and visit it. Little Italy, Chinatown, and plenty of other parts of the city are even made to look like different countries, and feature many of these countries' characteristics.","Besides all this culture, The City That Never Sleeps (another of New York City's nicknames, as many residents stay awake until the early hours of the morning) features gorgeous landscapes, a bustling economy, numerous landmarks, and much, much more.","Yankee Stadium, the home of the New York Yankees baseball team, is located in NYC, and because of baseball's considerable popularity in the US (and especially its popularity amongst New York locals!), is widely considered to be a must-see stop.","The Statue of Liberty, or the 305-foot-tall statue that represents freedom and justice in America, is also a \"huge\" tourist attraction located in NYC. Similarly, in a testament to The Big Apple's mentioned international importance, the United Nations Headquarters, or the meeting place of all the world's countries that're part of the peacekeeping group, also calls the city home.","It must be emphasized that the specified attractions are just the tip of the iceberg, as New York City features something for every type of person to see and do. NYC might not be the capital of New York State (that honor belongs to Albany, NY), but in many ways, it defines the state on an international level."],"q":[["The population of New York City is ...",["about the same as Los Angeles","over twice as large as that of Los Angeles","smaller than that of Los Angeles","about half of California's"],1],["New York is considered a \"global\" city because ...",["people from all over the globe live in and visit it","it is the capital of the USA","it is the largest city in the world","it has many airports"],0],["New York is called \"The City That Never Sleeps\" because ...",["the lights are never turned off","shops are open all night","many residents stay awake until the early hours","it is very noisy"],2],["Yankee Stadium is the home of a ... team.",["football","basketball","hockey","baseball"],3],["The Statue of Liberty represents ...",["peace and friendship","freedom and justice","wealth and power","history and culture"],1],["The capital of New York State is ...",["New York City","Buffalo","Albany","Washington"],2]]},{"t":"Pluto","n":174,"p":["Pluto is an interesting \"planet\" because it's actually not considered a planet anymore. It's a large object orbiting the sun--the tenth largest that we know of--but astronomers changed its classification to dwarf planet when better telescopes improved their ability to see far-away objects.","Pluto was discovered in 1930 and from then on became one of the nine planets all schoolchildren learned. It was the farthest out of the nine planets (light from the sun takes five hours to reach Pluto), so not much was known about it at first. As bigger and better telescopes became available, astronomers discovered that it is made of ice and rock and is about one-sixth the mass of the moon.","In the 1990s, astronomers made another discovery about Pluto. It was but one of several objects orbiting the sun in an area called the Kuiper belt. One of these objects, Eris, was even larger than Pluto. In 2006, the International Astronomical Union formally defined the term planet and excluded Pluto. Pluto and Eris are now known as dwarf planets."],"q":[["Today Pluto is classified as ...",["a dwarf planet","a planet","a moon","a star"],0],["Pluto was discovered in ...",["1990","2006","1903","1930"],3],["Light from the sun reaches Pluto in ...",["five minutes","five hours","five days","one hour"],1],["Pluto is made of ...",["gas and dust","iron","ice and rock","water"],2],["Eris is ...",["a moon of Pluto","a telescope","smaller than Pluto","larger than Pluto"],3],["In 2006, the International Astronomical Union ...",["formally defined the term \"planet\"","discovered the Kuiper belt","sent a telescope to Pluto","renamed Pluto"],0]]},{"t":"What Is Football?","n":169,"p":["Football means different things depending on where you live. If you're in the United Kingdom, South America, or Africa, it usually means soccer, or the sport where two opposing teams try to kick a round ball with alternating black and white pentagons into a net.","In England, football might also refer to rugby football. Rugby football is played with an oval ball. Players can run with the ball, pass it backwards, or kick it. Points are scored by getting the ball over the opposing team's goal line.","If you're in Australia, football means Australian-rules football, in which an oval ball is kicked between middle goal posts to score points. In Australian football, throwing is not allowed, so players must bounce the ball on the ground while running.","In the United States or Canada, football means gridiron football, where opposing teams take turns to throw or run with a brown oval ball with pointed ends until they can get a touchdown or get far enough to kick a field goal."],"q":[["In the United Kingdom, \"football\" usually means ...",["rugby","soccer","gridiron football","Australian-rules football"],1],["A soccer ball is described as ...",["brown and oval","oval with pointed ends","round with black and white pentagons","round and brown"],2],["In rugby football, players can pass the ball ...",["backwards","forwards","only by kicking","only with their heads"],0],["In Australian-rules football, ... is not allowed.",["kicking","running","bouncing the ball","throwing"],3],["In the United States and Canada, football means ...",["soccer","rugby football","gridiron football","Australian-rules football"],2],["In gridiron football, teams try to get ...",["a goal line","a touchdown","a home run","a net"],1]]},{"t":"My First Ski Trip","n":289,"p":["I had never skied in my life until last January when my friends invited me on a trip to a ski resort in Vermont. Before leaving, I made sure to pack all of my winter clothes, including sweaters, gloves, a hat, a scarf, thick pants, and a ski jacket. I also purchased new sunglasses that I could wear to shield my eyes from the bright glare of the white snow.","When we arrived at the ski resort, we stood in line and purchased tickets at the ticket window. The ticket granted us access to the slopes and ski lifts all afternoon. We also were able to rent ski equipment at an affordable price.","My friends wanted to help me learn to ski on the beginner slopes. At the top of the hill, they showed me how to maintain balance and coordination with ski poles in my hands. A few of my friends went down the slope before me so that I could watch their form and posture. I was nervous, but I appreciated their willingness to help me.","When I felt ready, I put my ski poles in the snow, leaned forward, and I began to slide down the slope. As soon as I began to move faster, I had already arrived at the bottom of the hill with success. My friends cheered me on to show their support.","I practiced a few more times on the smaller slopes. Later, I watched my friends go down the intermediate slopes on skis and snowboards. At the end of the day, we stopped for a meal inside the ski lodge. We ordered hot chocolate and some snacks as we sat by the fireplace and celebrated my first time skiing with success."],"q":[["The ski resort was in ...",["Colorado","Canada","Vermont","Switzerland"],2],["The writer bought new sunglasses in order to ...",["protect the eyes from the bright glare of the snow","look fashionable","replace a lost pair","give them to a friend"],0],["The tickets gave the friends access to the slopes and ski lifts ...",["for one hour","all week","all morning","all afternoon"],3],["The writer learned to ski on the ... slopes.",["intermediate","beginner","advanced","children's"],1],["Some friends went down the slope first so that the writer could ...",["take photos of them","rest for a while","watch their form and posture","rent equipment"],2],["At the end of the day, the friends ...",["went snowboarding at night","had hot chocolate and snacks by the fireplace","drove straight home","took another skiing lesson"],1]]},{"t":"Blue Whales","n":258,"p":["Have you ever wanted to see a whale close up? Blue whales are the largest mammal on earth, weighing up to 200 tons. They can grow up to 88 feet in length---larger than a jumbo jet!","Their migrations are some of the most fascinating in the world, with blue whales traveling for many miles to feed and give birth. Baby whales are called calves. The Blue whale gives birth to the calves off the coast of Southern Mexico, then together they travel more than 6,000 miles to Alaska's Bering Sea for their annual feast. The trip takes 2-3 months, and travels along the coast of Mexico, the West Coast of the United States and Hawaii along the route. Once they arrive in Alaska, they will feed on tiny, shrimp-like crustaceans called krill. They can eat up to four pounds of krill at a time.","You can find whales all over the globe, but the Blue whale mostly lives in the Northern Atlantic Ocean, North Pacific and Southern Hemisphere. Each year, these whales make a long trek, from the warm waters off the coast of Southern Mexico and California in the winter, to Alaska, Canada and Russia in the summer.","If you're near the coastline, you can see the whales for yourself. Whales force air from their blowholes just before they emerge from the water for graceful dives and playful flips. Look out for an eruption of water straight up into the air. Once you see that, it's only a few moments before you see this majestic creature emerge."],"q":[["Blue whales can weigh up to ...",["88 tons","100 tons","6,000 tons","200 tons"],3],["Baby whales are called ...",["calves","cubs","pups","krill"],0],["Blue whales give birth off the coast of ...",["Alaska","Hawaii","Southern Mexico","Canada"],2],["The trip to Alaska's Bering Sea takes ...",["2-3 weeks","2-3 months","6 months","a year"],1],["In Alaska, blue whales feed on ...",["small fish","sea plants","seals","krill"],3],["Before a whale emerges from the water, you can see ...",["an eruption of water straight up into the air","a dark shadow under the boat","birds flying in circles","large waves on the beach"],0]]},{"t":"American Holidays","n":533,"p":["Americans, like citizens of other countries, celebrate quite a few holidays, or publically acknowledged occasions wherein individuals (usually) receive time off from work and school to relax. Also like citizens of other countries, Americans celebrate some completely unique holidays, or holidays that are specific to their nation.","Some of the most popular holidays in America are:","New Year's Day/Eve: New Year's Day/Eve takes place on 31 December, and is, as its name suggests, a celebration of the New Year.","On New Year's Eve, most individuals celebrate by socializing with friends and family members, enjoying good-tasting foods and beverages, and playing games as they do so. New Year's Eve festivities culminate with the \"dropping of the ball\" at midnight, when, in New York City's Times Square, a gigantic, confetti-filled ball drops at exactly 12 AM, as the New Year officially begins; most individuals watch the ball dropping live on television.","New Year's Day celebrations are similar to those of New Year's Eve, except that they feature generally less \"partying\" and take place in the early morning and afternoon, as opposed to late at night. On New Year's Day, one might attend a brunch (meal between breakfast and lunch) celebration with friends and family members.","Valentine's Day takes place on 14 February, and is a celebration of the achievements and life of Saint Valentine, a priest in Ancient Rome. However, despite being rooted in religion, Valentine's Day is a public holiday in America, and is celebrated by all.","Seen as a day of romance, most individuals acknowledge Valentine's Day by purchasing candy, flowers, or a greeting card for their significant other (boyfriend, girlfriend, husband, wife, etc.), and perhaps going out for a special date.","Easter is a religious celebration of the Rising of Christ (in the Bible), and is a federal holiday (meaning most employees and students receive the day—and the following Monday—off from work).","Easter always takes place on Sunday, but its date varies annually; generally speaking, Easter is scheduled for some time in April. The holiday is widely celebrated with \"Easter baskets,\" or treat-filled baskets supposedly delivered by the \"Easter Bunny,\" the occasion's mythical figure.","Halloween (All Hollows' Eve) is an annual celebration that takes place on 31 October. Rooted in religion, the holiday is celebrated today by children (and some adults) dressing up in costume and trick-or-treating, or ringing the doorbells of a neighborhood's residents and receiving candy and other sweets from each residence. Adults often celebrate by attending Halloween parties.","Thanksgiving is an annual celebration that commemorates the Pilgrims' success in settling in North America, as well as everything today's citizens have to be thankful for. It takes place on the fourth Thursday in November, and is typically celebrated by families gathering together and eating home-cooked meals.","Christmas is an annual holiday that, religiously, celebrates the birth of Jesus. However, both Christians and non-Christians partake in the holiday by purchasing Christmas trees and, on Christmas morning, placing presents underneath these trees for other family members—particularly children. Children are usually told that these presents were left by Santa Claus, or the mythical figure that's said to bring gifts to all the good kids in the world.","Christmas takes place on 25 December."],"q":[["The \"dropping of the ball\" at midnight takes place in ...",["Washington","Times Square in New York City","Los Angeles","Chicago"],1],["A brunch is a meal between ...",["lunch and dinner","dinner and supper","breakfast and lunch","two parties"],2],["Valentine's Day is celebrated on ...",["14 February","31 October","25 December","31 December"],0],["According to the text, Easter always takes place on ...",["Monday","Thursday","Saturday","Sunday"],3],["On Halloween, children dress up in costumes and ...",["decorate trees","go trick-or-treating","watch fireworks","give presents to parents"],1],["Thanksgiving takes place on the ...",["last Friday in November","first Sunday in November","fourth Thursday in November","31 October"],2]]},{"t":"Sleepwalking","n":179,"p":["Lizzie heard her alarm and woke up. She was disoriented; she was on the couch but the sound of her alarm came from her room upstairs. Had she fallen asleep on the couch? No, she distinctly remembered getting in her bed. She must have been sleepwalking.","She was a little worried, so she looked up sleepwalking on the Internet. She found that sleepwalking is considered a parasomnia, an unwanted or undesirable behavior during sleep. Well, she certainly found it undesirable! What if she had done something dangerous while sleepwalking?","Sleepwalking occurs during the deepest stage of NREM (non-rapid eye movement) sleep and most often occurs in children. It can be caused by sleep deprivation, stress, or fever. Lizzie had stayed up late studying, so she figured stress and fatigue both contributed.","Sleepwalking is fairly common, Lizzie read. About 18% of the population does it at some point in their lives. It's not usually a cause for concern, but the sleepwalker can possibly get hurt or embarrassed by an episode. Lizzie decided she would not stay up late studying again!"],"q":[["When Lizzie woke up, she was ...",["on the couch","in her bed","at her desk","on the floor of her room"],0],["Lizzie was sure that she had not fallen asleep on the couch because she ...",["never sits on the couch","had set her alarm there","heard her mother","distinctly remembered getting in her bed"],3],["A parasomnia is ...",["a kind of medicine","an unwanted behavior during sleep","a stage of deep sleep","a sleeping problem only children have"],1],["Sleepwalking most often occurs in ...",["elderly people","students","children","athletes"],2],["Sleepwalking can be caused by ...",["sleep deprivation, stress or fever","eating late at night","too much sleep","watching television"],0],["About ... of the population sleepwalks at some point in their lives.",["8%","80%","28%","18%"],3]]}]]};
